WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.260
ACEMOGLU: Welcome
back, everybody.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:25.680
It's a great honor to
be here for MIT's 150th

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.810
to introduce you to the panel
on Macroeconomics, Growth,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.310
and Development.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.100
I think we've had a great start.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.490
It's kind of reminded
me how lucky I am

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.860
to have chosen this profession.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.530
I think that
economics is not only

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:50.590
an exciting discipline with
a great history of ideas

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.070
and questions, but it's
also amazingly fortunate

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.920
to have all of these
questions more or less open.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.700
So it kind of reminds me,
seeing all these debates

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.140
by the luminaries of
our field, that we still

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.560
have a lot of work to do ahead
of us, and lots of things

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.100
are still up for grabs,
not only in terms

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.118
of substantive questions,
but in terms of methodology,

00:01:15.118 --> 00:01:16.660
and how to approach,
and how to think

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:17.950
about these big questions.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.010
And I think the same
applies even more

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:25.240
to the topics of
this panel, which

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.000
is macroeconomics, economic
growth, and development.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.110
And I can't think
of a better panel

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.200
to kind of give us
perspectives on this.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.910
We're going to get started with
Robert Solow, who I don't think

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:40.900
needs any introduction.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:46.390
And he's going to give an broad
overview of the development

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.720
of macroeconomics
and economic growth

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.690
over the last 60 or so years.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.790
And then Peter Diamond
is going to follow up

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.190
with microfoundations
of macroeconomics,

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:00.010
in particular
emphasis on search.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:05.800
Robert Hall is going to
talk about diagnosing

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.000
more short-term issues,
or the long slump,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.750
which is somewhere
between short and long.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.140
And Esther Duflo is going
to talk about the where

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:17.650
micro and macro meet, and
in particular in respect

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.660
to development
economics, which is

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.850
one of the central
applications of the economics

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.670
discipline, which has
made tremendous progress

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.180
over the last few decades.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.640
So without further ado, I
will leave it to Bob Solow.

00:02:36.526 --> 00:02:39.412
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.837
SOLOW: No, I'm going
to speak from here.

00:02:46.837 --> 00:02:48.700
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.830
I think better sitting down.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.680
Like any good economist,
I asked myself

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.640
what my comparative advantage
was here this morning.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.880
And I decided it must be
related to my extreme age.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.180
So I thought I would say a few
words about how macroeconomics

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.090
has changed and worked
out during a very

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.700
long period of time.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.480
When I took my first
course in economics--

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:26.290
which, believe it or not, was
in 1940, that's 70 years ago--

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:30.490
there was no such thing
as macroeconomics,

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:35.740
and certainly no such
word as macroeconomics.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:39.070
There were courses
in money and banking.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:45.310
But all you learned that had
macro implications or scale

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.430
in money and banking courses was
you learned about the quantity

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.800
theory of money, and you
learned about banking crises.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.010
There were also courses
in business cycle theory.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:02.350
And they were certainly
macroeconomic in intention.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:09.550
But the whole history and
volume of business cycle theory

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:13.390
had no general
framework within which

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:19.180
you could combine and
compare various ideas

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.399
about fluctuations.

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:25.570
There was, in short,
in 1940, no inkling

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:29.170
of a theory of aggregate
income determination

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.860
that you could use to
sort of standardize

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:38.170
stories about fluctuations
in aggregate incomes.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:43.270
It's hard for people
now to visualize.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.650
But this frame of mind, without
any macroeconomic notion

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.340
at all-- what we would recognize
as macroeconomic notion--

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.550
persisted for a very long time.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:58.180
If you look at the Burns and
Mitchell Measuring Business

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.570
Cycles book, which was
published just after the war,

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:05.090
I suppose, 1946 or
so, you will see that.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:09.490
And I will tell you a story
which illustrates it, and dates

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.010
only from 1961.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:17.410
In 1961 and '62 were
the only period of time

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.170
in which I ever worked in
Washington, at the Council

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:22.660
of Economic Advisors.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:27.520
And we-- mostly Jim
Tobin, Art Oaken, an I,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.070
on this particular issue--

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:34.150
had some notions
which were put about,

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.480
about the evolution
of employment,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:42.340
about the nature of the status
of the labor market then,

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:44.980
in 1961 or '62.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:48.490
These notions were
strongly attacked

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:53.620
by Arthur Burns in
a bank newsletter

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.820
in a couple of articles
we found rather puzzling.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:01.150
Henry Wallach also
found them puzzling.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:04.540
Henry Wallach was a very good
economist, former professor

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.420
at Yale, and a member of
the Board of Governors

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.550
of the Federal Reserve System.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:15.250
And Henry Wallach went to see
Arthur Burns, and said to him,

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:18.700
and what trajectory
of GNP do you

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.860
imagine to be
consistent with what

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.980
you have said about the
evolution of employment?

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:29.020
And Burns's reply was, oh,
I don't think in GNP terms.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.890
So it was simply not
part of the vocabulary.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:37.300
The fact is that macroeconomics
in that sense in which I'm

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:41.920
talking about it was really
born in Keynes's general theory,

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:52.240
in 1936, as AC Pagout later
acknowledged very generously.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.800
And the Hansen Samuelson
multiplier accelerator model

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.640
and Lloyd Metzler's
inventory cycle model

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:04.180
are the earliest examples
of business cycle models

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:09.610
within the framework of
aggregate income determination,

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:14.860
at least aggregate income
determination on the demand

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.640
side.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:21.950
The word, macroeconomics,
I didn't try to date.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:24.580
It's almost certainly
due to Ragnar Frisch,

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.560
and at some later time.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:32.290
Now, if you leave aside
the particular formulations

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:35.230
that people think of
as, quote, "Keynesian,"

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:38.110
end quote, which I'm
not interested in here,

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:43.610
the important characteristics
of Keynes's economics

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:48.220
for this kind of history,
of historical view

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.860
of the evolution
of macro theory,

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:52.730
were two things in my mind.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:57.340
First, he wanted
to take seriously

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:02.140
the possible occurrence of
persistent aggregate excess

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:06.580
demand or aggregate
excess supply.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:11.230
And secondly, he
wanted a macroeconomics

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.920
that would keep closely
in touch with data

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:19.450
and would keep closely
in touch with events,

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:26.390
not just the second-order
moments of time series,

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.900
but actual events.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.660
It's no accident
that the evolution

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:33.610
of that kind of
economics went hand

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:37.210
in hand with the development of
the national income accounts.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:40.720
I think either of
those is inconceivable

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:42.799
without the other.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:46.510
Now of course the 1936
book, The General Theory,

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:49.960
is all about persistent
excess supply.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.560
But when the war broke
out, a little booklet

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:58.300
on how to pay for the war
was all about excess demand.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:03.370
By the way, someone once
accused Keynes of inconsistency.

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:06.320
And his reply was,
when the facts change,

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:07.610
I change my mind--

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:08.931
what do you do?

00:09:08.931 --> 00:09:10.895
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:20.530
Now, from 1950 to the
middle to late 1970s,

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.920
macroeconomics was all
Keynesian in the sense

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:27.760
that I have just mentioned.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:33.490
And most controversy
and discussion

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:39.400
was about accounting for facts,
not about points of theory.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:43.300
And macro economists were
interested in such things

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:45.770
as the measurement
of potential output,

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:49.510
the numerical value
of multipliers,

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:52.090
the numerical value
of the elasticities

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:56.530
of key behavioral relations,
especially the components

00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:01.210
of aggregate demand, like
consumer expenditure, business

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:05.180
investment expenditure, exports,
imports, the demand for money.

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:07.150
I want to be very
clear about this.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:12.370
The great divide in
macroeconomics in those days

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:16.420
was between monetarists
and Keynesians.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:21.970
But apart from debating
tactics and oneupmanship,

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.670
that whole argument
could be carried

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:30.610
on in terms of the shape and
variability of Hicks's IS

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:37.180
and LM curves, plus some
information about the supply

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.780
side and about
inflation dynamics.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.620
Those were all
empirical questions,

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.200
but they're hard
empirical questions,

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:51.010
so that there was lots of
room for a priori argument

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:54.700
and for ideology
to be dragged in.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:57.790
And of course they
did get dragged in.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:02.510
There was a sea change
in the late 1970s,

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:06.910
and that was primarily
event-driven.

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:10.060
What happened was a
period of inflation

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:14.860
without visible excess
aggregate demand.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:19.180
The word, stagflation,
was coined for that.

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:21.820
That is to say,
there were episodes

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:24.850
which could not be
understood without talking

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.880
about supply shocks.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:31.240
All the earlier attention--
except for growth theory,

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:32.260
of course--

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:35.630
had focused on
demand-side disturbances.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:42.790
So neither the Keynesian nor the
monetarist inheritors had any

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:48.580
really ready-made analysis
to deal with that kind

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:55.430
of inflation, driven initially
by the oil shocks, of course--

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:58.200
well, oil, and grain as well.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:01.580
So there was a temporary gap.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:04.250
The gap was filled
in fairly soon,

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:08.210
but it provided an
opening for opponents

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.000
of this way of doing
macroeconomics.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:18.530
Remember, I'm lumping Keynesians
and monetarists together.

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:24.500
And the opposition to this
way of doing macroeconomics

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:29.010
got its energy from two sources.

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:32.240
One, I think, you can
think of as ideology.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.000
Think of it as part
of the general turn

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.910
to conservatism that
took place then.

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:41.240
And the other was to
tap into the latent

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:47.940
desire of all economists
for theoretical neatness.

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:51.530
And this is not
something I just think,

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.230
this is something I
feel, since I have it

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:59.880
as much as anyone else.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:07.760
So after the 1970s, paying
close attention to events

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:09.500
got to be a bad thing.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:13.730
After the 1970s, to
describe something as ad hoc

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:17.330
became a pejorative term,
roughly the equivalent

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:18.570
of child molestation.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:20.105
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:26.975
And this is a very
serious matter.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:34.510
And all of the discussion
turned to microfoundations,

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:38.800
which is what Peter is going
to talk about in a minute.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:47.920
Bob Lucas made a very well-known
remark, a little bit later,

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:51.040
about how it would be
desirable to eliminate

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:54.160
the very distinction
between microeconomics

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:55.570
and macroeconomics.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:59.140
There should just be economics.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:03.280
I have to say I think that
was a profoundly foolish thing

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:09.410
to suggest, mainly
because it's impossible.

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:12.880
You can't answer the
questions macroeconomics

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:17.890
is asked to answer by
modeling the whole economy

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.920
as the interaction
of tens of millions

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:25.270
of households and millions of
firms and products, all of them

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:29.290
different, all of
them idiosyncratic.

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:33.070
Even the so-called agent-based
computer models that one sees

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:39.320
now are not trying
to explain events.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:41.470
It would be like trying
to design an airplane

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:45.670
molecule by molecule.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:50.050
For that reason,
what we mostly got,

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:55.930
apart from things
like search models

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:59.830
that Peter will talk
about, which arose out

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:05.540
of microeconomic, everyday
research, what we got

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:11.410
were primarily representative
agent-based models

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:17.680
where there's a set of
consistent preferences

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:21.370
that imposes some kind
of order and coherence

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:26.300
on observed the
macroeconomic outcomes.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:31.070
So I'm a crank about this.

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:33.070
And I won't go on about it.

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:38.050
Just to say that all that
talk about microfoundations

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:40.570
in practice turned out
to be a word for less

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:45.250
plausible macrofoundations
for macroeconomics,

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:49.180
and was more or less
divorced from the need

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:54.000
to account for macro events.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.870
This is related to the absence
of financial intermediation

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:00.910
of any realistic type.

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:04.150
By the way, nobody covered
themselves with glory

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:05.390
on that score.

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:07.810
But the logic of
representative agent models

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:11.290
almost precludes
serious autonomous

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:17.750
financial intermediation
with serious frictions.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:19.660
Now, I want to take
one more minute,

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:21.670
since I think I was
expected to say something

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:26.680
about growth theory in the
course of this discussion,

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:30.880
and I got wound up in what
I've just been telling you.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:36.430
So I'll take one or two more
minutes, and just say that,

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:38.450
so far as the future
is concerned--

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.200
and this fits in with some of
the micro discussion that we

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:43.870
had earlier in the morning--

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.480
what old growth
theory established

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:54.370
that the main force driving
trends in potential output

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:56.660
is total factor productivity.

00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:00.760
So the natural
direction of research

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:05.349
would be to
endogenize part or all

00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:10.480
of the evolution of total
factor productivity.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:17.380
And a very good, very
productive literature

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:22.270
went down two paths, one
using the accumulation

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:26.349
of human capital as the main
way of doing that endogenizing,

00:17:26.349 --> 00:17:29.200
and the other using
technological progress,

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:32.890
innovation, as the
way of doing that.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:35.830
And obviously that has to
be an important interaction

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:36.780
between those two.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:40.780
If you ask how much progress
has been made along those lines,

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:45.970
I think there's
some, but not much.

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:48.640
No one seems to
have developed a lot

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:53.320
in the way of stylized facts
or stylized near-facts,

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:58.160
near-theories along that line.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:00.940
Maybe the truly
exogenous element

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.040
is too large, maybe not.

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:08.050
But the thing that
has always nagged me

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:10.750
about this part
of the literature

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.860
is how hard it is to--

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:15.890
and this is another
micro/macro line,

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:18.040
and the last remark
I want to make--

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:23.650
how hard it is to
tell a coherent story

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:27.940
that has total factor
productivity on one side

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:34.250
and a history of concrete
innovation on the other.

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:39.160
How do we go in either
direction from the story that

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:45.250
involves stocks of capital,
human and physical,

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.670
and stocks of labor,
or flows of labor

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:54.730
of various degrees of skill,
and some undefined total factor

00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:57.130
productivity on one
side of the line,

00:18:57.130 --> 00:19:01.720
and the other side of the line
is the transistor, the cell

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:09.070
phone, the Kindle that
will replace us all,

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:12.100
and things like that.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:14.960
I think that's work
still to be done.

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:16.750
And it's going to be
a neat combination

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:18.610
of qualitative and quantitative.

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:19.297
Thanks.

00:19:19.297 --> 00:19:22.706
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:26.767 --> 00:19:27.600
ACEMOGLU: Thank you.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:28.560
Thank you, Bob.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.610
Peter Diamond is
going to speak next.

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:32.220
And I think he will
use the podium,

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:34.081
and he will have a presentation.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:45.050
DIAMOND: Jim Poterba
assigned me the topic

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:48.230
of the microfoundations
of macro.

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:51.110
And I, like everyone
else in the department,

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:56.060
always does what Jim suggests.

00:19:56.060 --> 00:20:03.320
When I agreed to speak here,
my memory flashed back.

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:08.810
I was a graduate student
at MIT's 100th anniversary.

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:12.170
And I actually remember
sitting in Kresge listening

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:16.640
to Harold macmillan, although I
could not, for the life of me,

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:19.310
tell you anything
he actually said.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:21.110
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:23.870
Even though we didn't
coordinate-- and this is,

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:26.300
I guess, part of Jim's genius--

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:31.340
my talk feeds directly
off Bob's in several ways.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:35.000
Growth theory was a hot
topic in lots of places,

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:38.750
but obviously particularly
at MIT, in the '60s.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:44.930
And growth theory was very odd,
probably unique at the time,

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:49.640
and still very unusual, in that
it was both a micro subject

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.830
and a macro subject.

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:55.970
And by subject, I mean
the people working on it,

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.820
some of my identified
themselves as macroeconomists,

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:03.170
some of them identified
themselves as microeconomists.

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:07.220
The modeling assumptions,
elements like that

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:09.620
were the same
across this divide.

00:21:09.620 --> 00:21:14.390
The uses being made of
it were not identical.

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:20.200
But it was something that
crossed that micro/macro

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:21.650
barrier.

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:28.060
And what made that work was a
lack of a deep contradiction

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:31.540
between the standard general
equilibrium model that

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:36.160
microeconomists grow up
with and start thinking from

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:40.750
and the basic
growth model, which

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:43.930
was going to be used for the
kinds of macro purposes--

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:48.370
not the short-run
stabilization issues

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:52.960
that Bob was talking about,
but was actually addressing

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:57.160
things where the micro
assumptions seemed

00:21:57.160 --> 00:22:01.300
to work for the macro ideas.

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:08.640
Since the '70s, we have seen
analysis based on the idea--

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:10.990
and this is the stuff
that Bob was criticizing.

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:13.390
I'm going to criticize
it a bit differently,

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:19.420
but we have the same warm
view of how good it is--

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:23.110
based on the idea
that the same dual use

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:27.460
would work for short-run macro
for business cycle analysis.

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:33.250
And of course Lucas and
Prescott are the key generators

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:35.320
of this literature.

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:39.070
The idea that a market-clearing
approach would work well

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:43.540
for short-run macro seemed to
me deeply implausible then,

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:47.570
and after watching it play
out over several decades,

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.470
every bit as implausible now.

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:55.750
If I could actually put Bob
Lucas in Bob Solow's camp

00:22:55.750 --> 00:23:02.740
for a moment, in 2003,
Bob Lucas gave an address

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:04.510
at a HOPE conference--

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:10.040
that's an acronym, not a
subject of the conference--

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:14.440
and he was commenting
on-- it's titled

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:17.800
"My Keynesian Education--"
and he was commenting

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:19.900
on the kind of
financial crisis we

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:23.690
had recently, Latin
America, Asia.

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:28.390
And he said, the models we know
and work well with have nothing

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:30.790
to say about this subject.

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:37.960
I take that to be an acceptance
that event-triggered analyses

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:41.710
call for different
kinds of modeling.

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:45.985
In the early '70s,
there were some--

00:23:45.985 --> 00:23:50.440
and I feel strongly Jim
Tobin was in that school,

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:54.580
and Bob and his remarks
tiptoed up to it--

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:59.200
who were opposed to seeking
a micro foundation for macro,

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:02.260
I think because they
thought it would end up

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:05.230
following the same
basically unproductive

00:24:05.230 --> 00:24:07.060
market-clearing line.

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:14.200
And Bob also was particularly
vocal on representative agents.

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:15.830
I had a different view.

00:24:15.830 --> 00:24:18.640
And I want to talk
about that today.

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:23.110
And not surprisingly, it's going
to connect to search theory.

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:26.680
The late '60s saw a rise of
a group of economists working

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:32.230
to construct a microfoundation
for macro out of search models.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:36.790
Different researchers had
different starting places.

00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:39.220
The desire for this
came from a sense

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:44.500
that standard macro modeling
involved micro concerns,

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:49.660
but did not incorporate them
in the same systematic way

00:24:49.660 --> 00:24:54.010
with underlying modeling
that was standard in micro.

00:24:54.010 --> 00:25:00.370
And the presumption was that
doing microfoundation modeling

00:25:00.370 --> 00:25:04.390
would give rise to new
insights and a better basis

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:08.560
for even short-run
macro phenomena.

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:11.500
The famous Phelps
volume had its eye

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:15.640
on getting foundations for the
Phillips curve, which related

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:17.860
unemployment and inflation.

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:21.640
My own starting place in the
'60s was rather different,

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:26.680
having cut my teeth initially as
a general equilibrium theorist

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:30.760
as an undergraduate
studying with Gérard Debreu.

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:33.610
It was a desire to
do micro better,

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:38.080
to overcome the limitations
in relevance coming

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:41.410
from the excessive
degree of coordination,

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:46.360
that the standard general
equilibrium model assumed.

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:50.350
I thought that a proper
microfoundation for macro

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:54.580
would also lead to a better
micro, as well as a better

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:56.380
macro.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:26:02.080
There are two key Keynesian
ideas that do not fit

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:05.170
comfortably in the basic
general equilibrium--

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:08.180
and I'll refer to it
as GE for shorthand--

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:09.580
model.

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:13.510
One is the large importance
of current income

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:16.330
in thinking about
things, exactly what's

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:18.850
behind the stimulus logic.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:22.210
And secondly the
stickiness of wages,

00:26:22.210 --> 00:26:26.620
the fact that wages
don't adjust quickly,

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:29.710
and that somehow
that's connected

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:33.730
with having a lot more
unemployed at some times

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:36.260
than at other times.

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:40.790
The challenge, as I saw it, was
to have a framework describing

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:44.240
the determination of an
equilibrium that had room

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.930
for both of these phenomena.

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:49.090
Alas, I can report
that we are still

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:52.360
working at trying to do this.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:56.410
And while there's a bit of
insight into these pieces,

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:59.200
not much.

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:03.190
And where we have
made a lot of progress

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:06.640
is on focusing on
the labor market

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:10.870
as a partial equilibrium
piece of thinking

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:14.290
about the whole economy.

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:19.870
Bob Solow, in 1964, gave
the Wicksell lectures.

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:24.910
And the Wicksell lectures
were, as I realize right now,

00:27:24.910 --> 00:27:28.930
a rebuttal to Arthur
Burns on why, at the time,

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:34.030
aggregate demand stimulation was
the right thing to be pursuing.

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:36.430
And the idea that
there was just a lot

00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:39.850
of structural unemployment out
there, and aggregate demand

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:45.700
wouldn't do anything about
it, that that view was wrong.

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:50.290
And that lecture goes
through a lot of discussion

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:54.280
of details of the
labor market, and does

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:56.650
them-- no surprise--
very intelligently,

00:27:56.650 --> 00:28:00.040
very insightfully,
but in a way that

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:06.850
was lacking an underlying
uniform theoretical

00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:09.270
underpinning.

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:15.390
And that same kind of debate
between structural unemployment

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:17.820
and the need for aggregate
demand stimulation

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:19.860
is going on today.

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:23.910
And today, the discussion
takes a rather different form

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:30.420
because search theory has grown
up, over the last 40 years,

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:33.510
to have a whole bunch
of different models

00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:36.990
to address different parts
of what goes on in the labor

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:39.990
market once you
assume what's going

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.920
on in the rest of the economy.

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:44.220
So it's partial equilibrium.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:49.650
And the starting place
for this is a focus

00:28:49.650 --> 00:28:55.080
not on the stock of unemployed
and the stock of the employed,

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:58.540
but labor market flows.

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:05.010
And here a 20-year average
calculates, from month

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:09.570
to month, what fraction of
unemployed workers in one month

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:11.610
are employed the
following month.

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:14.940
And the answer is 37%.

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:17.520
And what fraction of
unemployed workers

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:20.220
are actually out of the labor
force-- they're not employed

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:22.080
and they're not looking
for employment--

00:29:22.080 --> 00:29:23.440
the following month.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:26.250
And that's 33%.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:29.070
And if you look at the people
out of the labor force,

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:31.140
and there's of course
a vast number of them.

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:34.140
Many of them have
no interest in jobs.

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:36.460
Many of them are not
actively looking for jobs,

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:39.060
but actually are
available for work.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.150
And 4% of the "out
of the labor force"

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:45.720
people, labeled I, for
Inactive, in that figure,

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:49.410
are directly employed
a month later.

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:52.680
And 3 and 1/2%
are now officially

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.660
among the unemployed.

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:57.210
And what happens on
the employment side,

00:29:57.210 --> 00:30:00.870
well, roughly 2%,
every month, flow

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:04.980
from employment to unemployment,
and roughly 3% flow

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:09.810
every month from employment
to outside the labor force.

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:15.480
Over that 20-year period,
roughly six million workers

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:18.240
moved into employment
each month,

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:20.460
and roughly the same
number moved out

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:22.800
of employment each month.

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:26.660
And the change in
unemployment was

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:31.670
based on the small difference
between the large gross flows

00:30:31.670 --> 00:30:35.330
into and out of unemployment.

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:40.280
And the thrust of the modeling
and the empirical work that

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:45.650
has gone with it is to say,
let's focus on the flows

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:48.320
and assume the lessons
from the stocks

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:52.910
will be derived by
arithmetic from having

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:55.220
looked at the flows.

00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:59.330
Now, the US is a
bit of an outlier

00:30:59.330 --> 00:31:01.760
in the size of the flows.

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:07.050
But the flows are large in lots
of other countries as well.

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:10.580
Now, I've given you the
picture from the worker side.

00:31:10.580 --> 00:31:14.820
There's also a great deal of
work in a wonderful new data

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:20.510
set called JOLTS, the
analytical work led

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:23.540
by Steve Davis and
John Haltwanger,

00:31:23.540 --> 00:31:29.390
which looks at flows again
from the employer side.

00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:35.570
So you interview employers,
and this is here quarterly,

00:31:35.570 --> 00:31:40.220
not monthly, and you say,
do you have more employees

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:43.220
now than you did last quarter?

00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:46.430
And relative to
employment, the increase

00:31:46.430 --> 00:31:53.160
in employment where that happens
is 7 and 1/2% of employment.

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:55.550
And then you look
at other firms,

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:59.270
and they have fewer
employees than they had.

00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:02.520
And so that's labeled
job destruction.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:05.420
And you see it's
almost the same size.

00:32:05.420 --> 00:32:09.260
The decrease in employment
in firms having decreases

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:12.800
and the increase in employment
in firms having increases

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:15.240
is very close to each other.

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:16.850
And the difference
between them is

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:19.400
what's happening with
total employment.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:22.200
Again, the flows are huge.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:24.680
The change in stocks is small.

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:27.080
If you're looking
at the full pattern,

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:30.830
hiring is about twice as
large as job creation.

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:35.390
A worker leaves, gets replaced,
that's additional hires.

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:42.060
And if you look at workers
having left an employer,

00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:44.820
job destruction is part of that.

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:47.650
But quits is a big part of that.

00:32:47.650 --> 00:32:50.460
So you see quits and layoffs
are about the same size,

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.240
on average.

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:55.320
Although in the course
of a business cycle,

00:32:55.320 --> 00:32:59.860
quits and layoffs play
very, very differently.

00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:03.840
So these large flows,
and modeling the flows,

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:07.590
and modeling how they feed
into wage determination

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:12.100
is critical for two
kinds of questions.

00:33:12.100 --> 00:33:16.110
One question is we set
up unemployment insurance

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:17.550
programs.

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:21.030
They work at what I might
call ordinary times.

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:24.480
And they play a key
role in times like now,

00:33:24.480 --> 00:33:27.570
with a big recession,
high unemployment,

00:33:27.570 --> 00:33:32.640
where sometimes
automatically and sometimes

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:36.450
by explicit new legislation,
we get extended benefits,

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:39.398
we get changes in
benefit levels.

00:33:39.398 --> 00:33:41.940
And so the question is, how do
you want to think about those?

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:45.990
First of all, you
recognize you have

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:51.450
policies that are in place
in good times and bad times.

00:33:51.450 --> 00:33:55.260
Marginal tax rates
is another example.

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:56.970
Where in good times
we are focused

00:33:56.970 --> 00:34:00.570
on the impact of inefficiency,
you go into a recession,

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:04.200
they're a big part of the
built-in stabilizer that hold

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:06.846
down the size of a recession.

00:34:06.846 --> 00:34:08.429
So how do we want
to think about that?

00:34:08.429 --> 00:34:12.690
And a key part that came out
of formally modeling search

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:18.659
is the idea that this process
is not inherently efficient.

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:23.969
Unlike standard modeling of
markets in micro, where demand,

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:27.600
supply, the market clears,
and things are efficient.

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:29.159
People searching
for each other--

00:34:29.159 --> 00:34:32.520
workers looking for jobs,
employers looking for workers--

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:34.800
aren't in contact
with each other.

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:37.510
Their behavior
effects each other.

00:34:37.510 --> 00:34:40.980
And so there are externalities,
there are inefficiencies.

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:44.489
And you can't start thinking
about the role of unemployment

00:34:44.489 --> 00:34:46.770
without thinking about that.

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:49.500
And you can't start thinking
about the role of unemployment

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:53.909
without thinking about the
feedback onto aggregate demand,

00:34:53.909 --> 00:34:57.810
the piece that isn't
in the literature.

00:34:57.810 --> 00:34:59.970
So we're still a
long way from getting

00:34:59.970 --> 00:35:05.100
to where some people wanted to
start, with the Phillips curve.

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:11.760
Search is being built into
otherwise conventional macro

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:15.240
models, looking at things
like sticky prices, and wages,

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:17.490
and how they affect the economy.

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:22.650
But it's a
microfoundation piece, not

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:25.410
a full-blown
microfoundation model.

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:30.930
And I look forward to and
hope for a big expansion

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:34.170
in the range of
microfoundation models that

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:40.020
will be consistent with
the general equilibrium

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:42.660
views of thinking about
the whole economy,

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:47.190
and the Keynesian view
of recognizing the fact

00:35:47.190 --> 00:35:50.850
that we get, as Bob said, events
that really affect things.

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:53.640
And how the economy responds
to that is critical.

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:54.488
Thank you.

00:35:54.488 --> 00:35:58.472
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:01.908 --> 00:36:03.450
ACEMOGLU: Thank you
very much, Peter.

00:36:03.450 --> 00:36:06.360
And the next speaker
is Robert Hall, who

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:07.880
will talk about the long slump.

00:36:10.385 --> 00:36:12.010
HALL: Okay, well,
it's a great pleasure

00:36:12.010 --> 00:36:16.930
to be here to recognize this
important event in MIT's

00:36:16.930 --> 00:36:19.080
history.

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:21.490
MIT was very important
to me personally,

00:36:21.490 --> 00:36:27.710
first as a graduate student
in the mid-1960s, where

00:36:27.710 --> 00:36:31.220
my classmates have all
become just a hugely

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:35.750
important generation, including
Avinash Dixit and George

00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:37.560
Akerlof.

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:41.630
Avinash and I shared an
office, and I learned

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:43.280
an enormous amount from him.

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:47.450
In addition, MIT is
important to my family

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:51.380
in that my older sister was an
undergraduate here graduating

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:53.450
in 1963.

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:58.250
How many women were
in the class of 1963?

00:36:58.250 --> 00:37:01.840
I think it's, you
know, two hands' worth.

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:06.160
And she went on to and
continues an interesting career,

00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:08.170
most famous in the
family for the fact

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:11.260
that she worked
for the guy who put

00:37:11.260 --> 00:37:14.200
the @ sign in email addresses.

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:19.110
And my kids think that this
is absolutely legendary.

00:37:19.110 --> 00:37:23.080
More recently, my daughter
received a PhD in economics

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:29.910
from the economics department,
six years ago, in 2005.

00:37:29.910 --> 00:37:33.870
Now, the master of this
event, Jim Poterba,

00:37:33.870 --> 00:37:36.660
assigned me to talk
about something which he

00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:37.980
knew that I was thinking about.

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:40.680
It was not a random choice.

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:46.240
And that is to present
a diagnosis of what's

00:37:46.240 --> 00:37:48.010
happened in the US economy.

00:37:48.010 --> 00:37:52.420
I'll take the US economy as the
factual base for my discussion.

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:55.810
But similar things
happened in quite a number

00:37:55.810 --> 00:37:59.120
of other advanced countries,
and are continuing to happen.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:03.310
So you can think of this as
more of a worldwide analysis

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:08.320
than just focusing
on data from the US.

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:11.470
So let me give you
just a quick account

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:18.070
of the message of this
analysis and this diagnosis.

00:38:18.070 --> 00:38:23.110
There were three factors
that built up in the US

00:38:23.110 --> 00:38:26.590
economy in the past decade.

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:30.550
The first was a
tremendous splurge

00:38:30.550 --> 00:38:33.610
of spending for housing
and consumer durables,

00:38:33.610 --> 00:38:36.730
which left, coming into
the crisis and slump

00:38:36.730 --> 00:38:43.570
period, an overhang of
durables-- houses and cars

00:38:43.570 --> 00:38:45.910
in particular-- that
meant that we didn't need

00:38:45.910 --> 00:38:47.500
to build so many afterwards.

00:38:47.500 --> 00:38:52.390
And that overhang problem
is very much still with us.

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:55.510
The second was-- and closely
related to the first--

00:38:55.510 --> 00:39:02.160
was an overhang of high consumer
commitments to pay off debt.

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:08.670
It's not just that debt existed,
but as we went into the crisis,

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:12.230
lenders forced consumers
to repay their debt,

00:39:12.230 --> 00:39:16.050
and that had a huge effect
on the decline in consumption

00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:20.460
that occurred in
the contraction,

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:23.490
and it's still weighing
heavily on and preventing

00:39:23.490 --> 00:39:26.270
a brisk expansion.

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:28.890
And the final thing
is the weakness

00:39:28.890 --> 00:39:30.990
of financial institutions
that occurred

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:33.720
as a result of the
financial crisis itself

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:37.410
in the fall of 2008
resulted in a big increase

00:39:37.410 --> 00:39:39.870
in financial frictions.

00:39:39.870 --> 00:39:43.000
Now all of this interacted
with another factor,

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:49.350
which is that we quickly moved
to an economy that didn't have

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:52.210
the normal ability of
an economy to absorb

00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:54.450
a shock through
its interest rate.

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:56.770
We'd had a period of
low interest rates.

00:39:56.770 --> 00:40:01.410
The Fed responded to the
crisis almost immediately,

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:04.110
pushing interest
rates down to zero--

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:06.910
the interest rate that it
controls, the Fed funds rate.

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:11.920
But that wasn't nearly enough to
bring the economy back to life.

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:14.970
And this problem of
relatively little

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:17.190
headroom in monetary policy--

00:40:17.190 --> 00:40:20.320
because when you have low
interest rates to begin with,

00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:22.670
the stimulus you can get is
strictly limited by the fact

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:24.850
that interest rates
can't be negative.

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:27.110
And that gave us the
slump in the slump

00:40:27.110 --> 00:40:30.320
can be characterized most
efficiently in terms of data,

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:34.520
just as a big, rapid
increase in unemployment that

00:40:34.520 --> 00:40:40.190
started in 2007, but greatly
increased in 2008 especially

00:40:40.190 --> 00:40:42.620
toward the end of 2008.

00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:44.600
And we have this
very long period--

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:46.760
the dashed line
on the right there

00:40:46.760 --> 00:40:48.890
shows the normal
level of unemployment.

00:40:48.890 --> 00:40:51.470
Current forecasts don't have
us getting back to normal

00:40:51.470 --> 00:40:54.410
until 2015.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:58.010
So we still have a long
way to go according

00:40:58.010 --> 00:41:01.070
to expert forecasters.

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:02.607
I don't forecast.

00:41:02.607 --> 00:41:04.940
One of the reasons is that
Paul Samuelson, who taught me

00:41:04.940 --> 00:41:08.930
so many things, taught me,
if you have to forecast,

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:10.010
forecast often.

00:41:10.010 --> 00:41:11.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:15.370
And there is a very deep
wisdom in that remark, which

00:41:15.370 --> 00:41:17.120
says that forecasting,
if you get into it,

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:19.670
is always a full-time business.

00:41:19.670 --> 00:41:22.860
And if you want to be an idea
person, a research person,

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:24.770
you shouldn't also
be a forecaster.

00:41:24.770 --> 00:41:30.650
So this condemnation of my
beleaguered profession of being

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:35.188
a macroeconomist for having
failed to forecast the slump,

00:41:35.188 --> 00:41:36.230
well of course we didn't.

00:41:36.230 --> 00:41:37.580
We don't forecast.

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:39.030
We don't even do that.

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:42.260
So I think the real
test of our performance

00:41:42.260 --> 00:41:44.210
as a profession is
not our forecasting,

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:47.780
but rather are we doing a
good job of understanding what

00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:49.850
happened, and had
we done research

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:53.090
before that helps us understand?

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:56.945
And my reading as a biased--

00:41:56.945 --> 00:41:59.570
I have to say that I'm
biased, of course--

00:41:59.570 --> 00:42:01.958
as a biased reader of
that is that we actually

00:42:01.958 --> 00:42:03.500
did a lot of research
that turned out

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:05.510
to be highly
material, especially

00:42:05.510 --> 00:42:08.390
on the financial side.

00:42:08.390 --> 00:42:17.990
There is a paper by D. Bernanke,
Gertler, and Gilchrist,

00:42:17.990 --> 00:42:21.550
published in 1999,
that is a roadmap.

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:23.120
And that's not an obscure paper.

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:26.510
It's cited more
than 1,000 times.

00:42:26.510 --> 00:42:28.700
A lot was known
that reads directly

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:31.050
on what happened on
the financial side,

00:42:31.050 --> 00:42:33.890
and why financial
frictions rise when

00:42:33.890 --> 00:42:36.240
you have a financial
crisis in particular.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:37.940
So I want to defend
my profession

00:42:37.940 --> 00:42:39.648
against some of the
things that have been

00:42:39.648 --> 00:42:43.250
said, even by its friends here.

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:48.515
Okay, so the factors
leading up to the slump--

00:42:48.515 --> 00:42:50.390
I want to talk mostly
about the slump itself,

00:42:50.390 --> 00:42:53.720
but I found that everyone
wants to know my view of what

00:42:53.720 --> 00:42:56.242
caused these problems.

00:42:56.242 --> 00:42:57.950
First of all, there
was a dramatic easing

00:42:57.950 --> 00:43:04.340
of lending standards during the
first decade of the century.

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:06.500
That resulted in a
lot of new mortgages

00:43:06.500 --> 00:43:09.440
and bidding-up of
housing prices.

00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:12.560
At the same time,
so-called deregulation--

00:43:12.560 --> 00:43:16.310
and I condemn the notion that
it was ever even properly called

00:43:16.310 --> 00:43:18.170
deregulation--

00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:21.860
of financial
institutions occurred.

00:43:21.860 --> 00:43:26.900
I think that the one most
important failure of regulation

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:28.670
is easy to identify.

00:43:28.670 --> 00:43:33.410
In 2004 the SEC removed
its capital requirements

00:43:33.410 --> 00:43:36.230
on investment banks.

00:43:36.230 --> 00:43:38.660
So what are the names of
some investment banks?

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:39.490
Well, Lehman.

00:43:39.490 --> 00:43:40.792
What about Lehman?

00:43:40.792 --> 00:43:43.000
So the reason that Lehman
was able to do what it did,

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:45.760
which proved so
destructive, was that it

00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:49.870
had no limits on the amount of
leverage that it could adopt.

00:43:49.870 --> 00:43:52.210
And we learned that it
adopted a huge amount,

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:53.440
and then it fell apart.

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:55.810
As soon as asset prices
declined even a little,

00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:57.910
Lehman necessarily
fell apart because it

00:43:57.910 --> 00:44:01.160
was so highly levered.

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:03.850
So I think that the
regulatory side of this

00:44:03.850 --> 00:44:06.810
is a very important one.

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:09.590
I mentioned before, there was
a buildup of consumer debt,

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:12.830
and of a form with the
expansion of debt, in effect,

00:44:12.830 --> 00:44:15.830
people were borrowing
more to pay the interest

00:44:15.830 --> 00:44:17.540
on the debt they already had.

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:20.250
It was unsustainable.

00:44:20.250 --> 00:44:26.280
So the result was an
overshooting of housing prices.

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:29.600
And then-- it's a
very simple principle,

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:32.350
the housing prices, as
in most asset markets,

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:35.340
if it's gone up a
lot, it'll go down.

00:44:35.340 --> 00:44:36.410
And that's what happened.

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:38.818
And when it
happened, it affected

00:44:38.818 --> 00:44:40.610
these highly-levered
financial institutions

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:42.540
in a way we learned
so much about

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:46.370
in on September 15 of 2008.

00:44:46.370 --> 00:44:48.590
So there was a sudden
realization that capital had

00:44:48.590 --> 00:44:51.680
been depleted, there was
potential insolvency of a great

00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:58.440
many institutions, and panic,
and some very aggressive--

00:44:58.440 --> 00:45:00.020
and I think appropriate--

00:45:00.020 --> 00:45:03.950
government action, making up
for the failure of government

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:07.520
action in earlier years.

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:10.590
And all kinds of
institutions besides banks--

00:45:10.590 --> 00:45:13.850
investment banks, AIG's hedge
fund, money market funds--

00:45:13.850 --> 00:45:17.895
all proved to be vulnerable
to this decline in real estate

00:45:17.895 --> 00:45:18.395
prices.

00:45:21.840 --> 00:45:29.110
There was a big increase in
financial friction as a result.

00:45:29.110 --> 00:45:30.780
There was an
increase in spreads.

00:45:30.780 --> 00:45:32.265
Let me pause for a second.

00:45:32.265 --> 00:45:34.530
I'm going to de-synchronize
the slides here.

00:45:34.530 --> 00:45:37.260
This slide shows
this big increase

00:45:37.260 --> 00:45:42.720
in the relationship between
durables, household capital,

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:45.330
houses, and cars and
other consumer durables.

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:48.810
The red line on top,
you can see, rose a lot.

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:51.330
Nothing similar happened
on the business side.

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:53.655
The boom was very
focused on households

00:45:53.655 --> 00:45:56.040
and very focused on real estate.

00:45:56.040 --> 00:45:58.560
There wasn't much suspicious
or interesting going on

00:45:58.560 --> 00:46:00.240
in the business sector.

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:01.920
That was pretty much normal.

00:46:01.920 --> 00:46:05.040
What happened was a
big expansion of debt,

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:07.710
and a corresponding expansion
of building of houses,

00:46:07.710 --> 00:46:11.430
and a tremendous
splurge of buying cars.

00:46:11.430 --> 00:46:13.680
On the financial friction
side, one of the ways

00:46:13.680 --> 00:46:15.930
we like to measure
that is a spread, which

00:46:15.930 --> 00:46:18.930
is the difference between, for
example, what businesses charge

00:46:18.930 --> 00:46:21.660
on loans and what
their funds cost.

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:25.110
And that, this is really
key to the diagnosis

00:46:25.110 --> 00:46:27.850
that I'm talking about here.

00:46:27.850 --> 00:46:32.770
The spread rose in 2008,
and it's still high.

00:46:32.770 --> 00:46:36.580
We still have an economy with
excess financial friction

00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:38.140
relative to normal.

00:46:38.140 --> 00:46:40.580
And it hasn't gone
back to normal.

00:46:40.580 --> 00:46:42.230
And that's one of
the important factors

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:44.392
that's impeding recovery.

00:46:44.392 --> 00:46:46.100
If you look at that
on the consumer side,

00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:48.050
you see exactly the same thing.

00:46:48.050 --> 00:46:52.040
Credit card interest rates have
remained high at the same time

00:46:52.040 --> 00:46:57.890
that the cost of funds to
banks to provide credit

00:46:57.890 --> 00:47:01.120
to credit card borrowers
has declined substantially.

00:47:01.120 --> 00:47:03.350
And that's resulted in
increase in the spread.

00:47:03.350 --> 00:47:05.810
So there's another friction.

00:47:05.810 --> 00:47:09.920
In addition, there's
been a huge restriction

00:47:09.920 --> 00:47:12.800
in the availability of credit.

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:14.720
If you haven't used
it already, there's

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:18.170
a service at Google called
Google Insights that

00:47:18.170 --> 00:47:20.000
is just tremendously valuable.

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:22.430
And I looked at a
variety of searches.

00:47:22.430 --> 00:47:24.380
This shows that there
was an explosion

00:47:24.380 --> 00:47:27.770
of people looking
for information

00:47:27.770 --> 00:47:30.390
about withdrawal penalties.

00:47:30.390 --> 00:47:31.680
Now, what does that tell you?

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:34.070
It tells you that they'd
exhausted their normal ways

00:47:34.070 --> 00:47:38.000
of dealing with a financial
emergency at home,

00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:41.210
and had to go to things
like taking money

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:44.810
out of a retirement fund, which
involves a very substantial tax

00:47:44.810 --> 00:47:47.060
withdrawal penalty,
or taking money

00:47:47.060 --> 00:47:50.900
out of saving instruments that
have withdrawal penalties.

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:53.720
And you can see, at the normal
level, it was cruising along,

00:47:53.720 --> 00:47:55.440
and then suddenly it's shot up.

00:47:55.440 --> 00:47:59.100
And again, it hasn't
come back down very much.

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:01.520
And that's again, a
very important issue

00:48:01.520 --> 00:48:07.380
with respect to the
continuation of the slump.

00:48:07.380 --> 00:48:11.310
Okay, so what happened, turning
to the interest rate side,

00:48:11.310 --> 00:48:14.800
the Federal Reserve
responded very quickly.

00:48:14.800 --> 00:48:18.360
By the end of October, the
Fed had done everything

00:48:18.360 --> 00:48:20.940
that it can do with
conventional monetary policy.

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:25.170
It had pushed the fed funds
rate down to almost zero.

00:48:25.170 --> 00:48:27.520
And it's still there today.

00:48:27.520 --> 00:48:32.100
And forecasts shown
here on the right side

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:34.440
suggest that it's going
to remain at zero.

00:48:34.440 --> 00:48:36.750
And certainly the
most recent decision

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:39.120
was emphatically
to keep it at zero.

00:48:39.120 --> 00:48:42.870
We're far from the time when
we should begin to raise it.

00:48:42.870 --> 00:48:46.860
This scenario suggests
that next year it

00:48:46.860 --> 00:48:48.600
will begin rising,
but only by a little,

00:48:48.600 --> 00:48:50.940
and won't get back to
normal again until 2015,

00:48:50.940 --> 00:48:54.760
when the economy is thought
to be returning to normal.

00:48:54.760 --> 00:48:59.130
So we're in an extended
period when the Fed's hands

00:48:59.130 --> 00:49:01.740
are tied with respect
to its ability

00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:05.220
to expand the economy further.

00:49:05.220 --> 00:49:07.410
So that means that the
normal mechanisms that

00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:10.770
should operate in the economy,
of lowering interest rates,

00:49:10.770 --> 00:49:14.910
stimulating spending on, for
example, business capital

00:49:14.910 --> 00:49:17.730
to replace the fact
that people don't want

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:19.200
to spend any more on houses.

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:22.980
We built, in some sense,
way too many houses

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:24.720
through the year 2006.

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:27.810
We can rest on our ores with
respect to house production.

00:49:27.810 --> 00:49:30.090
We should be
producing other stuff.

00:49:30.090 --> 00:49:32.490
But that would require a
decline in interest rates.

00:49:32.490 --> 00:49:35.790
And that interest rate decline
cannot happen because interest

00:49:35.790 --> 00:49:37.480
rates can't be negative.

00:49:37.480 --> 00:49:40.770
And the result is the
normal equilibration process

00:49:40.770 --> 00:49:44.770
of the economy fails in
a situation like this.

00:49:44.770 --> 00:49:47.760
There's been a huge
outpouring of research

00:49:47.760 --> 00:49:50.940
that I've contributed to
on this question of what

00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:53.310
happens in an economy when
its interest rate can't

00:49:53.310 --> 00:49:56.250
go below zero.

00:49:56.250 --> 00:49:59.460
That's called a Zero Lower
Bound, or as we say, ZLB.

00:49:59.460 --> 00:50:03.810
There's now a whole branch
of macro called ZLB studies.

00:50:03.810 --> 00:50:10.210
And it's extremely
active right now.

00:50:10.210 --> 00:50:13.450
Okay, so what else
happened that's important?

00:50:13.450 --> 00:50:16.440
Fortunately it turned
out that nothing much

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:18.690
happened to inflation.

00:50:18.690 --> 00:50:20.820
Inflation cruised
at right exactly

00:50:20.820 --> 00:50:24.780
around 2%, which was the Fed's
desired level of inflation,

00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:26.700
for an extended period.

00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:28.860
At the beginning of the
crisis, it fell just a bit,

00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:31.150
by about almost exactly
a percentage point.

00:50:31.150 --> 00:50:34.670
And since then,
it's cruised at 1%.

00:50:34.670 --> 00:50:37.190
Now, you might think all
inflation is a bad thing,

00:50:37.190 --> 00:50:40.660
but that's not at all the
way macroeconomists think

00:50:40.660 --> 00:50:41.260
about it--

00:50:41.260 --> 00:50:43.510
quite the reverse.

00:50:43.510 --> 00:50:44.960
I've said-- on
several occasions,

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:47.050
but this is the most
appropriate one--

00:50:47.050 --> 00:50:52.030
Ben Bernanke would surrender
his MIT PhD in economics

00:50:52.030 --> 00:50:55.380
if we could have
3% inflation today.

00:50:55.380 --> 00:50:56.620
And why is that?

00:50:56.620 --> 00:50:58.320
It's because what
we really care about

00:50:58.320 --> 00:51:01.560
is the real interest rate,
which is the ordinary interest

00:51:01.560 --> 00:51:05.142
rate, the nominal interest rate,
minus the rate of inflation.

00:51:05.142 --> 00:51:06.600
What we'd really
like is for people

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:08.430
to perceive that
now is a great time

00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:10.440
to buy stuff instead of later.

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:11.940
Well, there's nothing
like inflation

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:14.640
to get that mentality going.

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:16.170
Normally we don't
like to see that.

00:51:16.170 --> 00:51:17.837
We want to keep
inflation under control,

00:51:17.837 --> 00:51:20.490
because it's a sign of
an overheated economy.

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:23.245
Well, we'd like a little bit of
that overheated mentality now.

00:51:23.245 --> 00:51:25.120
In the overheated economy,
people are buying.

00:51:25.120 --> 00:51:26.890
now instead of later.

00:51:26.890 --> 00:51:29.610
They're doing so guided
by financial incentives,

00:51:29.610 --> 00:51:30.783
the interest rate.

00:51:30.783 --> 00:51:32.700
We'd like a nice low
real interest rate, which

00:51:32.700 --> 00:51:34.455
says buy now instead of later.

00:51:34.455 --> 00:51:37.050
And that inflation
could deliver that.

00:51:37.050 --> 00:51:42.330
Well, you can't revoke
Ben Bernanke PhD.

00:51:42.330 --> 00:51:43.890
By the way, Franco
Modigliani once

00:51:43.890 --> 00:51:47.730
suggested that my PhD should be
revoked when I said something

00:51:47.730 --> 00:51:51.690
outrageous at a conference.

00:51:51.690 --> 00:51:53.740
But it didn't happen.

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:55.147
I still have it, I believe.

00:51:55.147 --> 00:51:56.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:56.520 --> 00:52:03.570
And so we're lucky that
we have this continuation

00:52:03.570 --> 00:52:07.470
of a moderate
amounts of inflation.

00:52:07.470 --> 00:52:11.400
Okay, so all these factors
that I've identified

00:52:11.400 --> 00:52:14.370
are going to continue as
far as anyone can see.

00:52:14.370 --> 00:52:16.230
Gradually, things are
going to get better.

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:19.140
The overhang of
overbuilding in one sector

00:52:19.140 --> 00:52:21.040
is going to gradually
come to an end.

00:52:21.040 --> 00:52:23.220
We're gradually going
to fill up those houses

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:26.710
and build more houses.

00:52:26.710 --> 00:52:30.720
The households are gradually
working down this excess debt

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:33.120
that they accumulated
in the last decade.

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:34.950
Financial frictions
are gradually

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:37.890
getting back to normal,
at least in some areas.

00:52:37.890 --> 00:52:40.920
And all that's going to
give us a gradual glide path

00:52:40.920 --> 00:52:42.600
of unemployment
back down to normal.

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:47.670
But another four
years, maybe more.

00:52:47.670 --> 00:52:51.113
With respect to policy,
the policymakers that

00:52:51.113 --> 00:52:52.530
were going to talk
this afternoon,

00:52:52.530 --> 00:52:56.880
I understand they're all still
waiting for their planes in DC.

00:52:56.880 --> 00:52:59.130
So maybe I can say a bit now.

00:52:59.130 --> 00:53:01.890
First of all, I think the Fed
has basically done all it can.

00:53:01.890 --> 00:53:03.360
It's made all the
right decisions

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:06.340
as far as I'm concerned.

00:53:06.340 --> 00:53:08.100
But there's not
much more it can do.

00:53:08.100 --> 00:53:10.580
And it is saying just
steady as you go,

00:53:10.580 --> 00:53:14.490
and not engaging in any
more attempts at expansion.

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:17.730
Because it's basically
done everything it can.

00:53:17.730 --> 00:53:20.850
There was a lot of interest
when the Obama administration

00:53:20.850 --> 00:53:23.880
came in.

00:53:23.880 --> 00:53:28.860
And MIT economists
were central there.

00:53:28.860 --> 00:53:32.550
Larry Summers, who basically
got his PhD from MIT

00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:35.187
because he took graduate
courses, including one for me--

00:53:35.187 --> 00:53:36.770
a memorable experience,
I have to say,

00:53:36.770 --> 00:53:39.870
and actually quite
a pleasant one--

00:53:39.870 --> 00:53:45.150
and Christy Romer, who has
an MIT PhD in economics,

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:48.893
were very instrumental in
designing early policies.

00:53:48.893 --> 00:53:51.060
One of them was to try to
take advantage of the fact

00:53:51.060 --> 00:53:54.570
that when the government
buys more stuff,

00:53:54.570 --> 00:53:57.870
every model agrees that
that stimulates the economy.

00:53:57.870 --> 00:54:00.330
Any economist who suggests
that that doesn't stimulate

00:54:00.330 --> 00:54:03.060
the economy hasn't
looked at a wide range

00:54:03.060 --> 00:54:04.560
of models, all of which agree.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:08.550
It's a numbered question,
but that number is positive.

00:54:08.550 --> 00:54:11.640
And furthermore-- and
this is really important--

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:13.740
in a zero lower bound
situation, which

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:16.680
is what existed when the
Obama administration came in,

00:54:16.680 --> 00:54:20.280
everyone also agrees that
the multiplier is higher

00:54:20.280 --> 00:54:23.702
when the zero lower
bound is in effect.

00:54:23.702 --> 00:54:25.410
So the numbers that
were put out for what

00:54:25.410 --> 00:54:28.050
you could get from increasing
government purchases, I think

00:54:28.050 --> 00:54:30.540
were about right.

00:54:30.540 --> 00:54:34.300
And that was a big effort
of the administration.

00:54:34.300 --> 00:54:40.020
But it failed completely, not
because the multiplier was low,

00:54:40.020 --> 00:54:42.270
but because it's just
impossible to get the government

00:54:42.270 --> 00:54:43.433
to buy more stuff.

00:54:43.433 --> 00:54:45.600
The only government agency
in the federal government

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:48.720
that bought any more
stuff was the Pentagon.

00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:51.030
But there was no
increase in other types

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:53.110
of federal government purchases.

00:54:53.110 --> 00:54:55.840
So we just didn't do it.

00:54:55.840 --> 00:54:59.830
And that's a repetition of
experiences we've had before.

00:54:59.830 --> 00:55:02.900
The only thing that's under
discussion now that would be

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:05.400
interesting-- and it's an idea
that quite a number of people

00:55:05.400 --> 00:55:06.480
have come up with--

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:09.180
is that if we could
use taxes to create

00:55:09.180 --> 00:55:11.430
the equivalent of
inflation, that

00:55:11.430 --> 00:55:13.710
would give us a huge stimulus.

00:55:13.710 --> 00:55:15.630
And it could be done
in a way that didn't

00:55:15.630 --> 00:55:18.280
have any budgetary impact.

00:55:18.280 --> 00:55:21.390
But that's an idea
that's borderline crank.

00:55:21.390 --> 00:55:24.060
So I don't think
it's going to happen.

00:55:24.060 --> 00:55:28.920
But it's an idea that, if
economists ran the show,

00:55:28.920 --> 00:55:30.780
it's probably something
we'd be doing.

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:32.340
And the last
comment I'll make is

00:55:32.340 --> 00:55:37.290
that all this happened
because of regulatory lapses.

00:55:37.290 --> 00:55:40.620
The most important thing, which
we've made almost no progress

00:55:40.620 --> 00:55:44.760
on, the Dodd-Frank bill,
only scratched the surface

00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:46.330
of what needs to be done.

00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:48.510
We need to have robust
financial institutions

00:55:48.510 --> 00:55:51.240
with lots of capital.

00:55:51.240 --> 00:55:53.950
When I say lots of capital,
I mean like 50% capital.

00:55:53.950 --> 00:55:56.250
There's no reason why we
shouldn't have a heavily

00:55:56.250 --> 00:55:59.100
capitalized financial system.

00:55:59.100 --> 00:56:03.750
And if that had been in place
and enforced, none of this

00:56:03.750 --> 00:56:05.040
would have happened.

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:10.770
It's entirely the result of a
very substantial enforcement

00:56:10.770 --> 00:56:14.370
policy lapse, of allowing
extremely highly-leveraged

00:56:14.370 --> 00:56:20.160
financial institutions to have
important roles in the economy

00:56:20.160 --> 00:56:22.020
without proper regulation.

00:56:22.020 --> 00:56:22.794
Thank you.

00:56:22.794 --> 00:56:26.112
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:30.628 --> 00:56:32.170
ACEMOGLU: So I agree
with Bob that we

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:34.330
should be careful in making
forecasts, especially about

00:56:34.330 --> 00:56:34.873
the future.

00:56:34.873 --> 00:56:36.790
But it seems that I just
made a good forecast.

00:56:36.790 --> 00:56:39.490
I asked Bob, so will you
be talking about policy?

00:56:39.490 --> 00:56:42.640
And he said, no, no, no, that's
the panel in the afternoon.

00:56:42.640 --> 00:56:45.280
I said, oh, I would have thought
you would talk about policy.

00:56:45.280 --> 00:56:46.630
And he did talk about policy.

00:56:46.630 --> 00:56:48.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:48.490 --> 00:56:51.160
So we're very happy to
have Esther Duflo, who

00:56:51.160 --> 00:56:54.340
is going to talk about, kind
of, an economic development.

00:56:54.340 --> 00:56:56.040
It's going to be a
gear change, I guess.

00:56:59.740 --> 00:57:02.080
DUFLO: I was trying to figure
out what could possibly

00:57:02.080 --> 00:57:04.790
be my comparative advantage.

00:57:04.790 --> 00:57:08.060
And I couldn't find one, except
that perhaps just before lunch

00:57:08.060 --> 00:57:10.220
you might want to
see some pictures.

00:57:10.220 --> 00:57:14.920
So here is a picture of Egypt.

00:57:14.920 --> 00:57:20.890
In 2010, Egypt got $1.3
billion in military aid

00:57:20.890 --> 00:57:23.800
and $250 million
in economic aid.

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:28.480
Egypt is the country that
gets the most aid from the US,

00:57:28.480 --> 00:57:31.590
except for Israel.

00:57:31.590 --> 00:57:34.128
Unemployment rate is about 25%.

00:57:34.128 --> 00:57:35.670
And as you can see
from this picture,

00:57:35.670 --> 00:57:39.240
there is deep, deep frustration
against a regime that

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:42.090
is both so corrupt
and extremely tired,

00:57:42.090 --> 00:57:48.090
both physically and
institutionally.

00:57:48.090 --> 00:57:52.890
If we had given all these aid
that went to Egypt to Africa,

00:57:52.890 --> 00:57:57.510
we could have given
$20 per year per child

00:57:57.510 --> 00:58:01.330
for every child under 5
who lives in Africa today.

00:58:01.330 --> 00:58:05.520
So far some people,
like Jeffrey Sachs,

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:10.290
this is a sign that we
could have ended poverty

00:58:10.290 --> 00:58:13.140
if we had had the
political will to do so.

00:58:13.140 --> 00:58:16.290
With this $20 per
child per year,

00:58:16.290 --> 00:58:20.910
you could have bought bed nets
to protect her from malaria,

00:58:20.910 --> 00:58:23.920
deworming medicine
to make her stronger

00:58:23.920 --> 00:58:28.560
and make her go to school
more, all of the immunization

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:30.570
that she would
need, and you would

00:58:30.570 --> 00:58:33.535
have plenty of cash left over.

00:58:33.535 --> 00:58:35.160
So if we don't do
that, it's because we

00:58:35.160 --> 00:58:39.210
don't want it because
our priorities are wrong.

00:58:39.210 --> 00:58:41.160
On the other side, we
have Bill Easterley,

00:58:41.160 --> 00:58:45.000
who's actually a graduate
from MIT, who says that, no,

00:58:45.000 --> 00:58:48.100
in fact this is a symptom, this
is a sign-- the Egypt case,

00:58:48.100 --> 00:58:52.770
as many others, is a sign
that aid, in general, fails,

00:58:52.770 --> 00:58:54.790
and that it's useless--

00:58:54.790 --> 00:58:57.870
or maybe worse than
useless, because it

00:58:57.870 --> 00:59:03.550
contributes to prop up regimes
which would have gone long ago.

00:59:03.550 --> 00:59:09.270
So the issue is how do
we say who is right.

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:11.890
And it is very difficult
to say who is right.

00:59:11.890 --> 00:59:15.660
If you look at
the aid to Africa,

00:59:15.660 --> 00:59:16.920
it's been increasing steadily.

00:59:16.920 --> 00:59:19.470
Those are the blue bars
here on the picture.

00:59:19.470 --> 00:59:23.910
Now of course growth has
not really been stellar.

00:59:23.910 --> 00:59:27.785
The GDP per capita
is the red line.

00:59:27.785 --> 00:59:29.910
So some, like Bill Easterley,
would conclude, well,

00:59:29.910 --> 00:59:32.030
it means that aid has
dramatically failed.

00:59:32.030 --> 00:59:33.530
Of course we have
no idea what would

00:59:33.530 --> 00:59:34.780
have happened without the aid.

00:59:34.780 --> 00:59:37.350
Maybe it would have gone
downwards, like here,

00:59:37.350 --> 00:59:40.390
or maybe it would have gone
upwards for that matter.

00:59:40.390 --> 00:59:42.990
And this is like-- the problem
is what Jerry Hausman said,

00:59:42.990 --> 00:59:46.260
we only have one world,
we only have one Africa,

00:59:46.260 --> 00:59:48.000
and we just don't
have a counterfactual

00:59:48.000 --> 00:59:52.620
to know what could possibly
have happened in this case.

00:59:52.620 --> 00:59:54.510
So when people don't
use this kind of figure,

00:59:54.510 --> 00:59:55.600
they use anecdotes.

00:59:55.600 --> 00:59:58.740
So one that is a very popular
one on both sides of the aisle,

00:59:58.740 --> 01:00:03.780
are Rwanda got tremendous amount
of aid after the genocide,

01:00:03.780 --> 01:00:05.220
and it did very well.

01:00:05.220 --> 01:00:06.880
And now the president,
Paul Kagame,

01:00:06.880 --> 01:00:10.080
wants to wean the country off
aid, and that's tough to do so.

01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:13.740
So one that is claimed as a
success both by Jeffrey Sachs

01:00:13.740 --> 01:00:15.930
and by Dambisa Moyo--

01:00:15.930 --> 01:00:17.910
by Jeffrey Sachs as
a success of aid,

01:00:17.910 --> 01:00:21.330
and by Dambisa Moyo as a poster
child for self-reliance--

01:00:21.330 --> 01:00:25.830
of course it's very
hard to arbitrate

01:00:25.830 --> 01:00:29.340
between this he said, she said,
because we have no idea what

01:00:29.340 --> 01:00:31.860
would have happened in
Rwanda if the policies

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:35.030
had been different.

01:00:35.030 --> 01:00:36.950
So what has happened in
development economics

01:00:36.950 --> 01:00:41.720
over the last few decades
is to try and move away

01:00:41.720 --> 01:00:44.090
from those big, big questions--

01:00:44.090 --> 01:00:46.010
can aid make a difference--

01:00:46.010 --> 01:00:49.520
to much, much smaller
questions, much more

01:00:49.520 --> 01:00:53.880
focused questions to which we
might possibly have an answer.

01:00:53.880 --> 01:00:57.830
So it's a little bit, in a
sense, the empirical equivalent

01:00:57.830 --> 01:01:02.090
of what [INAUDIBLE] said
is an MIT-style theory.

01:01:02.090 --> 01:01:06.800
This is, in my view,
MIT-style empirical work, too,

01:01:06.800 --> 01:01:10.200
not only MIT obviously.

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:12.970
So one of these questions,
for example, is malaria.

01:01:12.970 --> 01:01:16.070
Malaria is a focus question.

01:01:16.070 --> 01:01:17.990
I would agree it's
not a small question.

01:01:17.990 --> 01:01:18.960
It's pretty serious.

01:01:18.960 --> 01:01:22.340
It kills about 900,000
people every year,

01:01:22.340 --> 01:01:28.070
give and take a few, 91% of them
in Africa, 85% of them under 5.

01:01:28.070 --> 01:01:32.010
In fact, that's the leading
cause of under-5 mortality.

01:01:32.010 --> 01:01:33.270
So how do we go about it?

01:01:33.270 --> 01:01:34.890
Well, there's one
thing we do know

01:01:34.890 --> 01:01:37.710
from various experiments
done by doctors,

01:01:37.710 --> 01:01:41.700
is bed nets are an
effective way to prevent

01:01:41.700 --> 01:01:43.260
the spread of malaria.

01:01:43.260 --> 01:01:48.630
Long-lasting insecticide bed
nets are relatively cheap.

01:01:48.630 --> 01:01:51.360
For about $10, you
can manufacture one,

01:01:51.360 --> 01:01:56.560
ship it to Africa, and
teach a family to use it.

01:01:56.560 --> 01:01:58.560
The problem is that
$10 is still too

01:01:58.560 --> 01:02:01.340
much for many
African families when

01:02:01.340 --> 01:02:04.950
the GDP per capita for a country
like Kenya is about $300,

01:02:04.950 --> 01:02:06.150
for example.

01:02:06.150 --> 01:02:10.790
So only about 10% to
20% of children at risk

01:02:10.790 --> 01:02:13.020
sleep under a net.

01:02:13.020 --> 01:02:17.430
And of course as conventional
public finance would tell us,

01:02:17.430 --> 01:02:19.860
we should subsidize bed
nets, because malaria

01:02:19.860 --> 01:02:21.670
is highly contagious.

01:02:21.670 --> 01:02:24.830
So if I sleep under a net,
that helps the kid next to me.

01:02:24.830 --> 01:02:28.410
And in fact, the
externalities are not linear.

01:02:28.410 --> 01:02:31.980
Because the estimates are
that when 50% of people

01:02:31.980 --> 01:02:34.050
sleep under a net,
the mosquitoes

01:02:34.050 --> 01:02:36.960
don't have time to consummate
the parasite before they die,

01:02:36.960 --> 01:02:39.300
and therefore there is
no transmission anymore.

01:02:39.300 --> 01:02:42.440
So you can achieve
herd immunity from 50%.

01:02:42.440 --> 01:02:44.670
That would give you a
very strong argument

01:02:44.670 --> 01:02:46.800
for subsidizing net purchase.

01:02:46.800 --> 01:02:48.510
In fact, we might
even want-- there's

01:02:48.510 --> 01:02:50.223
no reason to stop at zero.

01:02:50.223 --> 01:02:52.140
It's not like inflation,
which for some reason

01:02:52.140 --> 01:02:54.218
is kept at zero--

01:02:54.218 --> 01:02:56.760
interest rates, sorry, which
for some reason is kept at zero.

01:02:56.760 --> 01:02:58.950
We could go and pay
people to use nets.

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:01.645
And maybe that's what
we should be doing.

01:03:01.645 --> 01:03:04.020
So this is a policy that hasn't
been argued, for example,

01:03:04.020 --> 01:03:08.010
by the WHO or by Jeff Sachs.

01:03:08.010 --> 01:03:12.300
But then there's always a new
economics to come forward.

01:03:12.300 --> 01:03:16.050
And there is this
very old principle

01:03:16.050 --> 01:03:17.897
in economics called
sunk cost fallacy, which

01:03:17.897 --> 01:03:19.980
is what you paid for
something shouldn't influence

01:03:19.980 --> 01:03:20.813
what you do with it.

01:03:20.813 --> 01:03:23.290
Because once you've paid
for it, you've paid for it.

01:03:23.290 --> 01:03:27.600
And these arguments in coming
from psychology and economics

01:03:27.600 --> 01:03:30.360
saying that maybe
the sunk cost fallacy

01:03:30.360 --> 01:03:34.200
isn't a fallacy to the
extent that people actually

01:03:34.200 --> 01:03:36.940
are influenced by it.

01:03:36.940 --> 01:03:40.380
So there could be what people
call a psychological sunk cost

01:03:40.380 --> 01:03:43.140
effect, which is unless
you pay for something, you

01:03:43.140 --> 01:03:46.380
consider it worthless.

01:03:46.380 --> 01:03:49.440
So the argument is that
if you give bed nets away,

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:51.480
people are not going to
use them as bed nets,

01:03:51.480 --> 01:03:58.480
but as wedding veils or fishing
nets, like on this picture.

01:03:58.480 --> 01:04:00.360
So the argument is very heated.

01:04:00.360 --> 01:04:02.340
It's a very, very
ideological argument

01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:03.570
with very strong position.

01:04:03.570 --> 01:04:06.735
Of course there are
dollars involved, but not

01:04:06.735 --> 01:04:08.110
very many dollars
when we compare

01:04:08.110 --> 01:04:09.630
to the US economy or anything.

01:04:09.630 --> 01:04:13.470
But a lot passion and ideology.

01:04:13.470 --> 01:04:17.610
And sitting from
where I sit, this

01:04:17.610 --> 01:04:19.350
is a bit strange
that this debate

01:04:19.350 --> 01:04:20.700
is dominated by ideology.

01:04:20.700 --> 01:04:24.420
Because it seems we could
pretty much resolve it.

01:04:24.420 --> 01:04:26.870
It seems that what
we do need to do

01:04:26.870 --> 01:04:29.910
to know whether bed nets
should be distributed for free,

01:04:29.910 --> 01:04:31.740
or we should pay
people to use them,

01:04:31.740 --> 01:04:34.440
or we should make
people pay a little bit,

01:04:34.440 --> 01:04:37.770
or make people pay full
fare, is number one,

01:04:37.770 --> 01:04:40.440
what's the price elasticity
of the demand for bed nets?

01:04:40.440 --> 01:04:43.560
Do we lose a lot of people if
we start charging for them?

01:04:46.660 --> 01:04:49.680
So that's the standard,
sort of economics 101

01:04:49.680 --> 01:04:52.660
question, the empirical
equivalent of that.

01:04:52.660 --> 01:04:55.860
Second is, if people
get bed nets for free,

01:04:55.860 --> 01:04:58.080
will they use them or
will they waste them?

01:04:58.080 --> 01:05:00.850
So that's the more sort of
behavioral sunk cost fallacy

01:05:00.850 --> 01:05:02.105
hypothesis.

01:05:02.105 --> 01:05:03.480
And third one,
which is something

01:05:03.480 --> 01:05:05.190
that also come
forward, is that are

01:05:05.190 --> 01:05:07.027
people are going to
be used to handouts.

01:05:07.027 --> 01:05:08.610
So you give them the
bed net for free,

01:05:08.610 --> 01:05:11.280
and maybe they use the bed
nets, but you can never ever

01:05:11.280 --> 01:05:16.170
sell them a bed net again,
or clothing, or school meals.

01:05:16.170 --> 01:05:19.490
They will always
expect handouts.

01:05:19.490 --> 01:05:21.390
And all of these
questions, in principle,

01:05:21.390 --> 01:05:23.720
you could answer by looking
at people at what people

01:05:23.720 --> 01:05:28.100
do, with the caveat that
you have the Jewish mother

01:05:28.100 --> 01:05:31.640
or Asian mother problem,
which is whoever decides

01:05:31.640 --> 01:05:35.360
to pay for a bed net is probably
not comparable than someone who

01:05:35.360 --> 01:05:38.910
will only get a bed net
if they get it for free.

01:05:38.910 --> 01:05:43.520
So a big movement in development
economics in the last 10 to 15

01:05:43.520 --> 01:05:47.960
years has been to try to
create a situation where people

01:05:47.960 --> 01:05:51.260
are exposed to different
conditions or treatment

01:05:51.260 --> 01:05:53.190
that are in fact comparable.

01:05:53.190 --> 01:05:55.520
And the way you do
this is the equivalent

01:05:55.520 --> 01:05:57.500
of the randomized
controlled trials

01:05:57.500 --> 01:05:59.990
that are used in
medicine to test

01:05:59.990 --> 01:06:01.930
the effectiveness of drugs.

01:06:01.930 --> 01:06:04.100
So to give you an
example here, this

01:06:04.100 --> 01:06:05.870
is an experiment
that was carried out

01:06:05.870 --> 01:06:09.560
by Pascaline Dupas, who is
assistant professor at UCLA

01:06:09.560 --> 01:06:12.110
and a former student of mine.

01:06:12.110 --> 01:06:15.020
What she did is to
randomly select households

01:06:15.020 --> 01:06:19.880
all over the place,
and offer them nets,

01:06:19.880 --> 01:06:24.590
either for free, or
with partial subsidies,

01:06:24.590 --> 01:06:26.780
or with full subsidies.

01:06:26.780 --> 01:06:30.200
And she randomly
broadcast her vouchers

01:06:30.200 --> 01:06:32.300
for the various
prizes for the nets

01:06:32.300 --> 01:06:34.130
so that people are
strictly comparable.

01:06:34.130 --> 01:06:36.630
And when we look
at that behavior,

01:06:36.630 --> 01:06:39.570
we know that it's the effect
of the price they had to pay,

01:06:39.570 --> 01:06:45.150
as opposed to any
differential selection.

01:06:45.150 --> 01:06:46.690
And what do we find?

01:06:46.690 --> 01:06:50.380
The first finding is that the
elasticity of demand for bed

01:06:50.380 --> 01:06:52.940
net is extremely strong.

01:06:52.940 --> 01:06:54.880
So for free, almost
everybody get it.

01:06:54.880 --> 01:06:57.250
As soon as you make people
pay just a little bit,

01:06:57.250 --> 01:06:58.780
the demand drops.

01:06:58.780 --> 01:07:02.470
And when you get to $3, which
is only about less than half

01:07:02.470 --> 01:07:06.770
of the full price, the
demand is basically all gone.

01:07:06.770 --> 01:07:09.200
So the price [INAUDIBLE]
is very high.

01:07:09.200 --> 01:07:11.080
So if you start
getting people to pay,

01:07:11.080 --> 01:07:13.400
you will lose a lot of people.

01:07:13.400 --> 01:07:15.230
Second question
is, are they going

01:07:15.230 --> 01:07:18.740
to use it if you make
them available for free?

01:07:18.740 --> 01:07:21.110
And there you find
sometimes no evidence

01:07:21.110 --> 01:07:24.830
for this psychological
sunk cost effect.

01:07:24.830 --> 01:07:34.260
People who paid for the bed net
and people who get it for free

01:07:34.260 --> 01:07:35.870
are about as likely to use it.

01:07:35.870 --> 01:07:37.860
I should say, it
means both there

01:07:37.860 --> 01:07:39.950
is no psychological
sunk cost effect,

01:07:39.950 --> 01:07:43.940
and the selection of people
who decide to purchase the net

01:07:43.940 --> 01:07:48.070
is not affected by the
price they have to pay.

01:07:48.070 --> 01:07:51.140
And the third question is
whether getting a free bed net

01:07:51.140 --> 01:07:53.240
discourages future purchases.

01:07:53.240 --> 01:07:57.030
And you find, in fact, if
anything, the opposite,

01:07:57.030 --> 01:08:00.470
which is people who got
their first bed net for free

01:08:00.470 --> 01:08:03.410
are somewhat more likely
to buy a second one when

01:08:03.410 --> 01:08:05.960
it's offered to them
about a month later

01:08:05.960 --> 01:08:08.190
at an average price.

01:08:08.190 --> 01:08:11.330
So it seems people do not
get used two handouts,

01:08:11.330 --> 01:08:13.130
they get used to nets.

01:08:13.130 --> 01:08:15.330
Which is why, in
the end, there is

01:08:15.330 --> 01:08:19.069
this learning and
information effect going on.

01:08:19.069 --> 01:08:20.790
So what do we get from this?

01:08:20.790 --> 01:08:23.180
Well number one, I think
we answer a policy question

01:08:23.180 --> 01:08:24.229
of key interest.

01:08:24.229 --> 01:08:26.750
When that paper of
Pascaline's came about,

01:08:26.750 --> 01:08:30.359
that sparked a lot of debate,
and that did change policies.

01:08:30.359 --> 01:08:33.170
For example, a big social
marketing organization PSA

01:08:33.170 --> 01:08:36.710
changed their policy and
started to give bed nets away.

01:08:36.710 --> 01:08:38.450
The government of
Kenya is also now--

01:08:38.450 --> 01:08:40.742
this experiment was done in
Kenya-- government of Kenya

01:08:40.742 --> 01:08:43.220
is distributing bed nets for
free in the maternal health

01:08:43.220 --> 01:08:44.899
clinic.

01:08:44.899 --> 01:08:46.819
Number two-- and
that's perhaps where

01:08:46.819 --> 01:08:50.359
it becomes more interesting
from sitting in an economics

01:08:50.359 --> 01:08:54.200
department-- is this
gives you a window on how

01:08:54.200 --> 01:08:59.310
and why the poor make the
decisions they are making.

01:08:59.310 --> 01:09:01.430
And here the big
puzzle is, given

01:09:01.430 --> 01:09:03.350
that people are
willing to use the net,

01:09:03.350 --> 01:09:06.590
seem to be valuing
them, why is it

01:09:06.590 --> 01:09:11.189
that they are so price-elastic,
at least at the first purchase?

01:09:11.189 --> 01:09:14.279
And that's not an isolated
thing for bed nets.

01:09:14.279 --> 01:09:17.100
The same thing is
true for immunization.

01:09:17.100 --> 01:09:20.380
Immunization is the
cheapest way to save lives.

01:09:20.380 --> 01:09:22.380
It's very, very cheap
to immunize a kid.

01:09:22.380 --> 01:09:24.779
The GAVI Alliance and
the Gates Foundation

01:09:24.779 --> 01:09:26.700
are pouring millions
and millions

01:09:26.700 --> 01:09:29.220
of dollars on the problem.

01:09:29.220 --> 01:09:31.090
And yet, in a country--

01:09:31.090 --> 01:09:33.990
there is about 20 million
children every year

01:09:33.990 --> 01:09:36.380
who don't get the
vaccination they should get.

01:09:36.380 --> 01:09:37.979
In a place like
Rajasthan, India,

01:09:37.979 --> 01:09:41.220
where this picture was
taken, 5% of children

01:09:41.220 --> 01:09:44.040
get the full set of
immunizations they should get.

01:09:44.040 --> 01:09:45.810
This is a tremendous waste.

01:09:45.810 --> 01:09:49.020
It's hard to imagine
a worse problem.

01:09:49.020 --> 01:09:51.330
And what's going
on here, well, we

01:09:51.330 --> 01:09:54.060
did an experiment
try to figure out

01:09:54.060 --> 01:09:57.210
whether small demand-side
incentive would

01:09:57.210 --> 01:10:02.957
make a difference in the
decision to get immunized.

01:10:02.957 --> 01:10:04.540
And what we find--
this is a baseline,

01:10:04.540 --> 01:10:08.070
you see almost no
one gets immunized--

01:10:08.070 --> 01:10:12.590
after a year, there
is some increase

01:10:12.590 --> 01:10:15.560
in immunization in the control
group where we did nothing.

01:10:15.560 --> 01:10:19.190
When we introduced some camps
to make immunization easier,

01:10:19.190 --> 01:10:21.302
some increase, to 17%.

01:10:21.302 --> 01:10:23.510
But then when you start
giving this small demand-side

01:10:23.510 --> 01:10:27.680
incentive-- we're talking
about a kilo of lentils--

01:10:27.680 --> 01:10:31.430
the full immunization rate
jumps from 17% to 38%.

01:10:31.430 --> 01:10:32.930
So it's a little
bit the same thing,

01:10:32.930 --> 01:10:34.730
where you get these
very, very high price

01:10:34.730 --> 01:10:37.970
elasticity for
preventive care, which

01:10:37.970 --> 01:10:41.580
contrast with huge
demand for curative care.

01:10:41.580 --> 01:10:43.250
And in fact, in
experiments I'm going

01:10:43.250 --> 01:10:46.460
to spare you the detail
of, very low price

01:10:46.460 --> 01:10:48.770
elasticity for curative care.

01:10:48.770 --> 01:10:51.800
So people are willing to
pay just about any price

01:10:51.800 --> 01:10:54.360
to cure their kid for malaria
once they have malaria,

01:10:54.360 --> 01:10:57.290
but they won't get a bed net.

01:10:57.290 --> 01:10:59.160
So that is kind of--

01:10:59.160 --> 01:11:02.510
that's the mystery to
what could be going on.

01:11:02.510 --> 01:11:06.560
Well, part of it could be
misinformation and mistrust.

01:11:06.560 --> 01:11:09.050
That's not only
developing countries.

01:11:09.050 --> 01:11:10.850
In this country
as well there are

01:11:10.850 --> 01:11:12.980
ways where people decide
that getting immunized

01:11:12.980 --> 01:11:17.130
is not so necessary anymore.

01:11:17.130 --> 01:11:19.110
Part of it could
be procrastination,

01:11:19.110 --> 01:11:23.670
a natural human tendency
to wait to do something.

01:11:23.670 --> 01:11:26.460
With preventive care,
the cost you have to pay,

01:11:26.460 --> 01:11:28.920
like going to the center
or paying for your bed net,

01:11:28.920 --> 01:11:31.780
is today, but the
benefit is in the future.

01:11:31.780 --> 01:11:34.800
So you might as well wait
a little bit to do it.

01:11:34.800 --> 01:11:37.380
And that's why
offsetting the small cost

01:11:37.380 --> 01:11:39.340
with a small benefit,
like for example

01:11:39.340 --> 01:11:42.860
in the case of the immunization
program, is so effective.

01:11:42.860 --> 01:11:46.140
Or maybe it's a combination
of the two things.

01:11:46.140 --> 01:11:48.870
Regardless of what it is,
understanding exactly what's

01:11:48.870 --> 01:11:52.080
going wrong is key to
design effective health care

01:11:52.080 --> 01:11:55.620
policy in developing countries.

01:11:55.620 --> 01:11:58.020
For example, what
are the lessons

01:11:58.020 --> 01:11:59.530
that come out of each one?

01:11:59.530 --> 01:12:01.430
One is absenteeism.

01:12:01.430 --> 01:12:05.670
This is a closed health
center in Udaipur District,

01:12:05.670 --> 01:12:09.300
63% of centers are closed
when they should be open.

01:12:09.300 --> 01:12:12.870
Absenteeism is very [INAUDIBLE]
and it's amazingly costly,

01:12:12.870 --> 01:12:15.300
because it introduces
both mistrust

01:12:15.300 --> 01:12:19.950
and increases those little costs
that stand in the way of people

01:12:19.950 --> 01:12:22.300
doing the right thing.

01:12:22.300 --> 01:12:25.300
Secondly, information
is important,

01:12:25.300 --> 01:12:27.060
but we have to recognize
that information

01:12:27.060 --> 01:12:29.790
is going to be difficult.
Because learning

01:12:29.790 --> 01:12:32.670
about preventive care
is very, very hard

01:12:32.670 --> 01:12:34.590
for people who had no
high school biology,

01:12:34.590 --> 01:12:37.570
and even for people who
had high school biology.

01:12:37.570 --> 01:12:38.800
So there is a lot of trust.

01:12:38.800 --> 01:12:42.840
And I think the right
message and way of diffusing

01:12:42.840 --> 01:12:45.590
the information is hard.

01:12:45.590 --> 01:12:50.870
Thirdly, we cannot possibly
overemphasize the importance

01:12:50.870 --> 01:12:52.970
of making things easy.

01:12:52.970 --> 01:12:56.040
And this is something that,
from the comfort of our lives,

01:12:56.040 --> 01:12:57.540
we don't fully realize.

01:12:57.540 --> 01:12:59.840
For example, we don't
realize that water

01:12:59.840 --> 01:13:04.520
comes chlorinated in
our tap free of charge

01:13:04.520 --> 01:13:07.440
when we moan about the fact that
the poor don't boil the water,

01:13:07.440 --> 01:13:09.620
when we never had
to boil our water.

01:13:09.620 --> 01:13:12.410
We don't realize that we have
to immunize our children,

01:13:12.410 --> 01:13:14.700
because otherwise we
can't send them to school.

01:13:14.700 --> 01:13:17.190
So that is a very
powerful incentive.

01:13:17.190 --> 01:13:20.270
And then we-- we meaning
the policymakers, experts,

01:13:20.270 --> 01:13:21.320
et cetera--

01:13:21.320 --> 01:13:23.960
make big speeches about
the importance of the poor

01:13:23.960 --> 01:13:26.780
really understanding
what they need to do,

01:13:26.780 --> 01:13:28.990
and we shouldn't bribe
them to do the right thing.

01:13:28.990 --> 01:13:31.820
We are constantly
bribed and helped

01:13:31.820 --> 01:13:34.710
to do the right
thing in our lives.

01:13:34.710 --> 01:13:37.310
So why did I spend so
much time on that question

01:13:37.310 --> 01:13:38.510
of health care?

01:13:38.510 --> 01:13:42.980
Just because it's, I think, a
good illustration of the modus

01:13:42.980 --> 01:13:45.750
operandi in development
economics today.

01:13:45.750 --> 01:13:49.640
Take a pretty focused
question, and bring it

01:13:49.640 --> 01:13:51.560
all your attention
and your focus,

01:13:51.560 --> 01:13:54.760
and try to answer it very well.

01:13:54.760 --> 01:13:57.230
The question, of course,
is whether when we do that,

01:13:57.230 --> 01:14:00.350
we sort of give up on the
large questions of how

01:14:00.350 --> 01:14:02.960
our economy's going
to grow, and are we

01:14:02.960 --> 01:14:04.490
going to get treat
of poverty one

01:14:04.490 --> 01:14:06.890
day, whether we have
completely exploded

01:14:06.890 --> 01:14:09.950
the problems into a
myriad of small problems,

01:14:09.950 --> 01:14:14.810
and we are too far
from the big answer.

01:14:14.810 --> 01:14:19.280
So obviously if I thought
the answer was yes, maybe

01:14:19.280 --> 01:14:22.280
I would not have phrased
the question that way.

01:14:22.280 --> 01:14:27.050
I do think this very patient,
step-by-step approach

01:14:27.050 --> 01:14:29.960
is a very productive
way of trying

01:14:29.960 --> 01:14:36.200
to understand how the poor
behave, why they behave

01:14:36.200 --> 01:14:39.560
the way they do, and
therefore how we can possibly

01:14:39.560 --> 01:14:44.710
help them get out of
poverty trap or get going.

01:14:44.710 --> 01:14:46.340
And it's not going to be easy.

01:14:46.340 --> 01:14:49.400
Because this is from
the accumulation

01:14:49.400 --> 01:14:51.470
of these experiments
and experiences

01:14:51.470 --> 01:14:55.850
that a set of general principles
can maybe start to appear.

01:14:55.850 --> 01:15:00.020
But something that-- it
needs a lot of replication.

01:15:00.020 --> 01:15:02.930
Something that works somewhere
may not work elsewhere.

01:15:02.930 --> 01:15:04.820
It also means that
in a lot of cases,

01:15:04.820 --> 01:15:07.580
you have to be like Keynes
and change your mind when

01:15:07.580 --> 01:15:08.390
the facts change.

01:15:08.390 --> 01:15:11.990
Because the facts
easily change on you.

01:15:11.990 --> 01:15:15.410
But I don't think there
is another way, really.

01:15:15.410 --> 01:15:18.380
And in a sense I think
there is an analogy

01:15:18.380 --> 01:15:21.950
between this way of proceeding
and 20th and 21st-century

01:15:21.950 --> 01:15:27.140
medicine, where you have a
back and forth between trying

01:15:27.140 --> 01:15:29.480
to understand what a
particular molecule do,

01:15:29.480 --> 01:15:32.810
and then going to a big project
like the Human Genome Project,

01:15:32.810 --> 01:15:36.680
where the two kind of feed
off each other to develop

01:15:36.680 --> 01:15:37.880
medicines.

01:15:37.880 --> 01:15:41.810
And there are no miracle
cure to anything in medicine,

01:15:41.810 --> 01:15:44.690
there are just thousands
and thousands of drugs,

01:15:44.690 --> 01:15:46.730
and a better and
better understanding

01:15:46.730 --> 01:15:47.630
of the human body.

01:15:47.630 --> 01:15:52.650
And together, that amounts
for millions of lives saved.

01:15:52.650 --> 01:15:55.130
So I also wouldn't
like you to be

01:15:55.130 --> 01:15:58.620
fooled by the apparent
modesty of the objective.

01:15:58.620 --> 01:16:00.890
Deworming sounds sort of--

01:16:00.890 --> 01:16:02.440
very, very small thing.

01:16:02.440 --> 01:16:05.330
It's take a pill twice a year.

01:16:05.330 --> 01:16:07.700
How can that be
possibly important.

01:16:07.700 --> 01:16:10.940
It turns out that deworming--

01:16:10.940 --> 01:16:13.190
kids who have been
dewormed for one more

01:16:13.190 --> 01:16:16.785
year than comparable kids,
when they were in school,

01:16:16.785 --> 01:16:24.005
earn 20% more every year
when they are adult. 20%

01:16:24.005 --> 01:16:27.850
more every year is a lot of
years of good growth in Kenya.

01:16:27.850 --> 01:16:29.060
This study was done in Kenya.

01:16:29.060 --> 01:16:32.000
And there has not been that
many good years of good growth

01:16:32.000 --> 01:16:33.970
in Kenya anyway.

01:16:33.970 --> 01:16:36.800
So these are actually things
that can quantitatively

01:16:36.800 --> 01:16:39.490
be extremely important
here and now,

01:16:39.490 --> 01:16:41.505
and have policy implications.

01:16:41.505 --> 01:16:45.410
A few years ago, I was in Davos,
started this Deworm the World

01:16:45.410 --> 01:16:49.105
organization, which is kind of
get all of these rich people

01:16:49.105 --> 01:16:50.480
to give money to
deworming, which

01:16:50.480 --> 01:16:56.690
is sort of pleasant
anyway, and gets this--

01:16:56.690 --> 01:16:58.130
making some progress
in deworming

01:16:58.130 --> 01:17:01.670
as many kids as possible.

01:17:01.670 --> 01:17:04.820
So where are we?

01:17:04.820 --> 01:17:06.410
Well, in a sense,
I think there is

01:17:06.410 --> 01:17:09.890
a pretty clear moral
case for trying

01:17:09.890 --> 01:17:11.750
to help here and now if we can.

01:17:11.750 --> 01:17:14.570
I think there is an
efficiency case as well.

01:17:14.570 --> 01:17:18.140
It seems like a
considerable waste

01:17:18.140 --> 01:17:21.830
not to do the maximum we can
to help kids like these kids

01:17:21.830 --> 01:17:24.530
get educated and healthy.

01:17:24.530 --> 01:17:28.010
My personal view is
that it can't possibly

01:17:28.010 --> 01:17:32.840
be bad for growth, in that
when and where the growth spark

01:17:32.840 --> 01:17:37.700
starts, people who aren't
properly educated and healthy

01:17:37.700 --> 01:17:41.780
will be probably more likely
to take advantage of it.

01:17:41.780 --> 01:17:44.870
Even if it were wrong, I
don't really see a strong case

01:17:44.870 --> 01:17:48.170
not to do the maximum we can.

01:17:48.170 --> 01:17:51.240
And in a sense, I don't
think we have a choice.

01:17:51.240 --> 01:17:53.840
I think that's a political
imperative as well.

01:17:53.840 --> 01:17:55.830
An go back to Egypt--

01:17:55.830 --> 01:17:59.390
I think the poor are not
going to sit silently

01:17:59.390 --> 01:18:01.610
while we are trying
to figure out

01:18:01.610 --> 01:18:05.390
how we can make the economic
[INAUDIBLE] or the labor market

01:18:05.390 --> 01:18:06.960
work better.

01:18:06.960 --> 01:18:11.360
And I'll finish with Roosevelt.
I replaced the world "country,"

01:18:11.360 --> 01:18:14.910
which he was using in 1932.

01:18:14.910 --> 01:18:17.300
"The world needs and,
unless I mistake its temper,

01:18:17.300 --> 01:18:20.240
the world demands bold
persistent experimentation.

01:18:20.240 --> 01:18:22.700
It is common sense to
take a method and try it.

01:18:22.700 --> 01:18:25.310
If it fails, admit it
frankly and try another.

01:18:25.310 --> 01:18:27.428
But above all, try something.

01:18:27.428 --> 01:18:28.970
The millions who
are in want will not

01:18:28.970 --> 01:18:31.490
stand by silently
forever while the things

01:18:31.490 --> 01:18:34.550
to satisfy their needs
are within easy reach.

01:18:34.550 --> 01:18:35.404
Thank you.

01:18:35.404 --> 01:18:39.356
[APPLAUSE]

01:18:47.260 --> 01:18:49.420
ACEMOGLU: Thank you
very much to all four

01:18:49.420 --> 01:18:53.470
panelists for these
very interesting and

01:18:53.470 --> 01:18:54.940
thought-provoking comments.

01:18:54.940 --> 01:18:57.610
I would like to see whether
any one of the panelists

01:18:57.610 --> 01:19:00.760
would like to make any
follow-on comments.

01:19:00.760 --> 01:19:05.430
And if not, I think
we have a little bit

01:19:05.430 --> 01:19:09.250
of time for questions.

01:19:09.250 --> 01:19:11.590
And the microphones
are up front again.

01:19:11.590 --> 01:19:14.740
Please come to either
of these two sides.

01:19:20.260 --> 01:19:21.510
AUDIENCE: Thank you, everyone.

01:19:21.510 --> 01:19:25.010
My name is [INAUDIBLE] from
Northeastern graduate student.

01:19:25.010 --> 01:19:27.360
Last week, one of
my students asked me

01:19:27.360 --> 01:19:29.390
a simple question in
Professor [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:29.390 --> 01:19:34.160
test book about why the human
capital is never decreasing.

01:19:34.160 --> 01:19:36.390
I found it very difficult
to answer such questions,

01:19:36.390 --> 01:19:39.160
not because I don't know
the answers in test book,

01:19:39.160 --> 01:19:43.610
but because of my
experience before two years.

01:19:43.610 --> 01:19:45.320
I was hired by the
Chinese government

01:19:45.320 --> 01:19:47.570
to do a survey in
Jiangsu Province

01:19:47.570 --> 01:19:49.640
about economical growth.

01:19:49.640 --> 01:19:51.650
So I spent half a
year to do survey.

01:19:51.650 --> 01:19:54.200
But what I see is all
the local governments

01:19:54.200 --> 01:20:00.020
they do is introduce capital,
introduce technique, and spend

01:20:00.020 --> 01:20:02.360
a lot of money to
develop educations.

01:20:02.360 --> 01:20:05.130
Because China always
lack of these things.

01:20:05.130 --> 01:20:08.780
But the problem is that
all the foreign factories

01:20:08.780 --> 01:20:12.560
invest in China, they just
use China as a factory, not

01:20:12.560 --> 01:20:13.490
industry.

01:20:13.490 --> 01:20:17.670
China become a part of
productions for those firms.

01:20:17.670 --> 01:20:22.700
As a result, it has no such
demand for human capital

01:20:22.700 --> 01:20:25.810
and techniques because
there's no design department,

01:20:25.810 --> 01:20:28.610
there is no [INAUDIBLE].

01:20:28.610 --> 01:20:31.700
So as a result, all the
people have a high education

01:20:31.700 --> 01:20:34.040
level, the [INAUDIBLE]
to outside,

01:20:34.040 --> 01:20:35.840
not stay at those
countries, stay

01:20:35.840 --> 01:20:38.850
on those less developing areas.

01:20:38.850 --> 01:20:42.190
So for those less
developing countries,

01:20:42.190 --> 01:20:45.590
even they spend a lot of money,
time to develop a technique,

01:20:45.590 --> 01:20:47.480
develop human
capital, as a result,

01:20:47.480 --> 01:20:49.830
they never get
benefit from that.

01:20:49.830 --> 01:20:54.830
So my question is that, based
on Professor Solow's series,

01:20:54.830 --> 01:20:58.490
the difference [INAUDIBLE]
of economic growth,

01:20:58.490 --> 01:21:03.860
they don't have stable
solutions in the equations.

01:21:03.860 --> 01:21:07.340
So whether it is a time for
us to reconsider the power

01:21:07.340 --> 01:21:10.100
and function of human
capital techniques

01:21:10.100 --> 01:21:11.330
for developing countries.

01:21:11.330 --> 01:21:15.020
Because these powerful
factories never

01:21:15.020 --> 01:21:16.840
belong to those
developing countries.

01:21:16.840 --> 01:21:17.340
Thank you.

01:21:21.990 --> 01:21:22.710
SOLOW: I'm sorry.

01:21:22.710 --> 01:21:24.840
I would like to reply
if it's directed at me.

01:21:24.840 --> 01:21:26.090
HALL: Let me try to translate.

01:21:26.090 --> 01:21:28.978
SOLOW: I don't hear very well,
and I could not make it out.

01:21:28.978 --> 01:21:30.270
AUDIENCE: I'm sorry about that.

01:21:30.270 --> 01:21:32.010
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:21:32.010 --> 01:21:35.048
ACEMOGLU: Bob Hall, I
think, will say something.

01:21:35.048 --> 01:21:37.393
[LAUGHTER]

01:21:40.193 --> 01:21:41.610
HALL: Well, a
one-sentence version

01:21:41.610 --> 01:21:46.512
is, what's the role of human
capital in growth theory?

01:21:46.512 --> 01:21:48.480
SOLOW: Oh.

01:21:48.480 --> 01:21:51.924
[LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE]

01:21:55.500 --> 01:22:00.240
I said earlier that,
for the long run,

01:22:00.240 --> 01:22:02.520
it's extraordinarily
important-- supremely

01:22:02.520 --> 01:22:06.510
important-- to understand
the evolution of total factor

01:22:06.510 --> 01:22:10.380
productivity, with an eye, of
course, to asking the question

01:22:10.380 --> 01:22:15.090
what a country or
some other policy unit

01:22:15.090 --> 01:22:21.780
can do to accelerate the growth
of total factor productivity.

01:22:21.780 --> 01:22:26.250
And the literature has produced
these two interrelated paths

01:22:26.250 --> 01:22:29.700
by which total factor
productivity improves.

01:22:29.700 --> 01:22:35.430
One is by improving
the skills of people,

01:22:35.430 --> 01:22:40.170
and the other is by
technological innovation.

01:22:40.170 --> 01:22:43.740
And they can't be
independent, because most

01:22:43.740 --> 01:22:46.200
technological
innovations require

01:22:46.200 --> 01:22:50.730
some set of specific skills
which need to be imparted.

01:22:50.730 --> 01:22:56.340
And as production gets more
technologically sophisticated,

01:22:56.340 --> 01:22:59.955
greater skills are needed.

01:23:03.000 --> 01:23:07.950
It's very hard, from
the sorts of time series

01:23:07.950 --> 01:23:15.260
that we have, to isolate the
relative contribution of these

01:23:15.260 --> 01:23:19.710
or any other particular source
of total factor productivity

01:23:19.710 --> 01:23:23.600
that you would like
to think about.

01:23:23.600 --> 01:23:31.440
There is certainly no
doubt that human capital

01:23:31.440 --> 01:23:37.230
is extremely important, not
only in the growth of advanced

01:23:37.230 --> 01:23:43.730
countries, but also in the
development of poor countries.

01:23:43.730 --> 01:23:52.350
And the big thing that China
has brought to the world economy

01:23:52.350 --> 01:24:00.370
is a very large source
of low-wage labor,

01:24:00.370 --> 01:24:03.820
first of all
employed in functions

01:24:03.820 --> 01:24:08.020
that require very little in
the way of human capital.

01:24:08.020 --> 01:24:17.410
And then the question is, can
the investment in education

01:24:17.410 --> 01:24:22.030
facilitate, make possible, and
even make necessary the move

01:24:22.030 --> 01:24:26.170
to more and more sophisticated
lines of production?

01:24:26.170 --> 01:24:29.513
In the case of China,
that appears to be so.

01:24:29.513 --> 01:24:30.930
I don't know whether
I've answered

01:24:30.930 --> 01:24:33.930
the question in particular.

01:24:33.930 --> 01:24:36.940
It's certainly the question
I would have asked myself.

01:24:36.940 --> 01:24:39.750
ACEMOGLU: So we'll have
a couple more questions.

01:24:39.750 --> 01:24:41.760
If you can make the
questions relatively brief,

01:24:41.760 --> 01:24:46.622
because I think we
have limited time.

01:24:46.622 --> 01:24:47.580
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

01:24:47.580 --> 01:24:49.580
My name is Karen,
and my question

01:24:49.580 --> 01:24:51.830
is for Professor Diamond.

01:24:51.830 --> 01:24:55.730
And I know you were nominated by
President Obama to the Federal

01:24:55.730 --> 01:24:57.690
Reserve Board once again.

01:24:57.690 --> 01:25:02.330
So I want to hear your
opinion on the effect of QE2.

01:25:02.330 --> 01:25:05.960
And I once heard you say you
are a believer of the market

01:25:05.960 --> 01:25:07.290
and capitalism.

01:25:07.290 --> 01:25:09.880
And I want to share
your opinion that

01:25:09.880 --> 01:25:14.390
do you believe the US economy
can work its own way out when

01:25:14.390 --> 01:25:17.570
the effect of QE2
wears off this time.

01:25:17.570 --> 01:25:20.360
Thank you so much.

01:25:20.360 --> 01:25:22.220
DIAMOND: The first
rule I was told

01:25:22.220 --> 01:25:24.920
after I was nominated
for the first time,

01:25:24.920 --> 01:25:27.060
and the second time,
and the third time--

01:25:27.060 --> 01:25:29.210
which I think should
put me in the Guinness

01:25:29.210 --> 01:25:31.790
Book of World Records--

01:25:31.790 --> 01:25:33.860
is don't answer
questions like that,

01:25:33.860 --> 01:25:35.270
it will just get in the way--

01:25:35.270 --> 01:25:36.080
[LAUGHTER]

01:25:36.080 --> 01:25:38.250
--of your being confirmed.

01:25:38.250 --> 01:25:41.510
So let me skate around it.

01:25:41.510 --> 01:25:46.490
First of all, Bob Hall said
the Fed has pushed the interest

01:25:46.490 --> 01:25:50.600
rate down to zero, and there's
very little more it can do.

01:25:50.600 --> 01:25:53.510
QE2 is something more.

01:25:53.510 --> 01:25:59.940
And I don't think anyone thinks
of it as a huge deal by itself.

01:25:59.940 --> 01:26:03.890
But I share the view that has
been expressed by the existing

01:26:03.890 --> 01:26:04.750
governors--

01:26:04.750 --> 01:26:07.370
I think I can say
that in public--

01:26:07.370 --> 01:26:13.760
that it is helpful in
the right direction.

01:26:13.760 --> 01:26:18.140
But I share the view
implicit in what Bob said,

01:26:18.140 --> 01:26:21.800
that it isn't as
powerful as an ability

01:26:21.800 --> 01:26:26.914
to lower interest rates
2%, 3%, or 4% more.

01:26:26.914 --> 01:26:29.342
ACEMOGLU: Okay, one--
so I think we should--

01:26:29.342 --> 01:26:29.842
yeah.

01:26:32.970 --> 01:26:34.978
AUDIENCE: Bill Carlson, '68.

01:26:34.978 --> 01:26:37.020
And I'm going to ask this
question of you, Daron,

01:26:37.020 --> 01:26:39.870
and you can allocate
it to the panel.

01:26:39.870 --> 01:26:42.340
You've published research
on dual labor markets,

01:26:42.340 --> 01:26:45.090
the so-called "good jobs,
bad jobs" literature.

01:26:45.090 --> 01:26:48.390
And I'm curious whether this
distinction between good jobs

01:26:48.390 --> 01:26:51.540
and bad jobs is so
fundamental that it should

01:26:51.540 --> 01:26:54.660
be in the microfoundations
of macro models

01:26:54.660 --> 01:26:57.150
whose intent is to predict
the effect of stimulus

01:26:57.150 --> 01:26:59.820
policies, especially when you
take into account Williamson's

01:26:59.820 --> 01:27:02.163
work on the structure of firms.

01:27:02.163 --> 01:27:04.080
ACEMOGLU: I think that's
a wonderful question.

01:27:04.080 --> 01:27:05.670
But I'm not going
to answer it myself.

01:27:05.670 --> 01:27:07.240
And I'll see whether--

01:27:07.240 --> 01:27:09.450
Peter.

01:27:09.450 --> 01:27:14.010
DIAMOND: Let me just
say that a major part

01:27:14.010 --> 01:27:17.130
of the flows in the
slides that I showed

01:27:17.130 --> 01:27:21.130
are flows of employed
people going to other jobs.

01:27:21.130 --> 01:27:24.030
And so it's a quit, and
it's a re-employment,

01:27:24.030 --> 01:27:27.930
and that that
generally represents

01:27:27.930 --> 01:27:32.460
an increase in the
efficiency in the economy.

01:27:32.460 --> 01:27:35.010
You could call it moving
from a bad job to a good job,

01:27:35.010 --> 01:27:37.860
but it may be a
series of small steps.

01:27:37.860 --> 01:27:41.370
That's a nice vocabulary for
something that's a continuum.

01:27:41.370 --> 01:27:46.770
And there is a significant part
of the literature exploring

01:27:46.770 --> 01:27:50.190
what drives it,
why it disappears.

01:27:50.190 --> 01:27:53.460
Quits disappear we
go into a recession.

01:27:53.460 --> 01:27:56.650
As Bob has noticed,
when the quits are gone,

01:27:56.650 --> 01:27:59.760
then the replacements
disappear, and that that

01:27:59.760 --> 01:28:01.320
affects hiring rates.

01:28:01.320 --> 01:28:08.350
So this issue, in a different
vocabulary, is very much there.

01:28:08.350 --> 01:28:13.350
It's not in the simplest
basic starter model

01:28:13.350 --> 01:28:15.960
but I think it's the
kind of thing that

01:28:15.960 --> 01:28:19.440
is recognized as
an important part

01:28:19.440 --> 01:28:24.750
of the cost of a recession, that
these things aren't happening.

01:28:24.750 --> 01:28:28.350
HALL: So David Autor, in the
MIT economics department,

01:28:28.350 --> 01:28:30.300
has done some very
interesting research

01:28:30.300 --> 01:28:32.190
that suggests that
we really need

01:28:32.190 --> 01:28:36.660
to think about three groups
in the labor market, not two.

01:28:36.660 --> 01:28:39.450
There's one group of people
who have very little training

01:28:39.450 --> 01:28:42.090
and basically work
with their hands--

01:28:42.090 --> 01:28:45.120
they do gardening, and
they take care of people--

01:28:45.120 --> 01:28:48.840
and they're doing all right
by historical standards.

01:28:48.840 --> 01:28:51.240
Then there's the people
who have graduated

01:28:51.240 --> 01:28:57.810
from MIT, who are ready to go
out and develop new products,

01:28:57.810 --> 01:29:00.600
and do intellectual
problem-solving work.

01:29:00.600 --> 01:29:02.740
And they're doing
extremely well.

01:29:02.740 --> 01:29:05.010
And then there's
a very sad group

01:29:05.010 --> 01:29:09.900
in the middle who are
high school graduates,

01:29:09.900 --> 01:29:18.310
but are the ones whose jobs have
been taken away, so to speak,

01:29:18.310 --> 01:29:20.130
by computerization.

01:29:20.130 --> 01:29:23.018
And they have suffered
the biggest disadvantage.

01:29:23.018 --> 01:29:25.060
So rather than talking
about a dual labor market,

01:29:25.060 --> 01:29:29.430
I think the next step is the
three-part, trinary labor

01:29:29.430 --> 01:29:33.360
market, to get a good picture of
what the US labor market looks

01:29:33.360 --> 01:29:35.316
like.

01:29:35.316 --> 01:29:36.800
ACEMOGLU: Yes, please.

01:29:36.800 --> 01:29:40.790
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE],, Sloan
School of Management, '74.

01:29:40.790 --> 01:29:43.410
I took a course
from Professor Hall.

01:29:43.410 --> 01:29:45.795
I don't really remember
what I learned.

01:29:45.795 --> 01:29:48.353
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:48.353 --> 01:29:50.770
HALL: That's good, because I
don't remember what I taught.

01:29:50.770 --> 01:29:51.780
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:51.780 --> 01:29:54.090
AUDIENCE: But I remember
it was very important.

01:29:54.090 --> 01:29:56.117
And I wish I had
learned more of it.

01:29:56.117 --> 01:29:57.550
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:57.550 --> 01:30:02.360
My question sparks
from your chart that

01:30:02.360 --> 01:30:05.030
showed the spreads
on lending, which

01:30:05.030 --> 01:30:08.390
are very high for both
consumers and corporates,

01:30:08.390 --> 01:30:09.860
and they remain high.

01:30:09.860 --> 01:30:14.450
And the observation I
want to insert into that,

01:30:14.450 --> 01:30:19.160
and then ask a question, is
that the shadow banking system

01:30:19.160 --> 01:30:22.370
is effectively on its back.

01:30:22.370 --> 01:30:25.510
Structured financings
are not happening,

01:30:25.510 --> 01:30:28.700
and they're not likely to
happen in any way in the size

01:30:28.700 --> 01:30:33.680
that they're used to, for a
variety of reasons, mostly

01:30:33.680 --> 01:30:36.410
good but many bad,
overregulation

01:30:36.410 --> 01:30:38.900
being one of the bad ones.

01:30:38.900 --> 01:30:44.300
Given the lack of
lending that is

01:30:44.300 --> 01:30:46.040
taking place in
the high spreads,

01:30:46.040 --> 01:30:52.370
how do you see the
evolution of the decrease?

01:30:52.370 --> 01:30:55.710
The observation I had about
the structured finance market,

01:30:55.710 --> 01:30:58.370
it basically has slowed
the velocity of money.

01:30:58.370 --> 01:31:01.790
And I wonder to what
extent you see reduction

01:31:01.790 --> 01:31:05.090
in spreads and incentives
that might be given to reduce

01:31:05.090 --> 01:31:07.060
those spreads from lenders.

01:31:07.060 --> 01:31:14.230
HALL: So on the business side,
the bulk of American business

01:31:14.230 --> 01:31:18.880
is not dependent on borrowing
from financial institutions.

01:31:18.880 --> 01:31:20.470
There's relatively
little leverage.

01:31:20.470 --> 01:31:26.120
Where I live, corporations
don't borrow from banks at all.

01:31:26.120 --> 01:31:28.510
Quite the country, they
accumulate almost mindlessly.

01:31:28.510 --> 01:31:30.490
Like Apple-- Apple
has $50 billion

01:31:30.490 --> 01:31:35.620
in short-term instruments
on its balance sheet,

01:31:35.620 --> 01:31:38.440
not borrowing, but lending.

01:31:38.440 --> 01:31:42.407
And the shareholders are getting
a little restless about that.

01:31:42.407 --> 01:31:43.990
Of course there are
certain industries

01:31:43.990 --> 01:31:46.840
that are credit-dependent.

01:31:46.840 --> 01:31:51.340
And they are still definitely--
there's no question

01:31:51.340 --> 01:31:55.030
that there's an impairment of
credit flows in certain areas.

01:31:55.030 --> 01:31:56.530
I think, though,
and I think there's

01:31:56.530 --> 01:31:58.750
been a big shift of the
thinking of macroeconomists

01:31:58.750 --> 01:32:03.940
in this direction, that the real
credit-dependent sector is not

01:32:03.940 --> 01:32:06.670
business, but it's households.

01:32:06.670 --> 01:32:09.983
And the real questions
are the squeeze

01:32:09.983 --> 01:32:12.400
that's gone on in the household,
where they were permitted

01:32:12.400 --> 01:32:14.780
to borrow large amounts
in the last decade,

01:32:14.780 --> 01:32:18.190
and then forced to repay
that over the last few years.

01:32:18.190 --> 01:32:21.340
That, in terms of GDP--

01:32:21.340 --> 01:32:22.060
the way I think--

01:32:26.230 --> 01:32:29.440
is very much larger
than what I would say

01:32:29.440 --> 01:32:34.660
is a relatively marginal issue
with respect to businesses.

01:32:34.660 --> 01:32:38.200
Most GDP arises
in businesses that

01:32:38.200 --> 01:32:40.370
have direct access
to credit markets,

01:32:40.370 --> 01:32:43.690
if they need credit at all--

01:32:43.690 --> 01:32:44.590
or finance.

01:32:44.590 --> 01:32:47.830
They're mostly equity-financed.

01:32:47.830 --> 01:32:51.100
The US business sector
is not highly levered.

01:32:51.100 --> 01:32:52.240
Only certain sectors are.

01:32:52.240 --> 01:32:54.310
Airlines are.

01:32:54.310 --> 01:32:56.960
We used to say that
auto companies were.

01:32:56.960 --> 01:32:59.800
But generally speaking, leverage
is not a factor in business.

01:32:59.800 --> 01:33:04.310
It is a huge factor in
financial institutions.

01:33:04.310 --> 01:33:06.498
But how important that is
for the business sector

01:33:06.498 --> 01:33:07.790
I think is still up in the air.

01:33:07.790 --> 01:33:11.080
It's the household side that
I think it deserves the most

01:33:11.080 --> 01:33:13.150
attention today.

01:33:13.150 --> 01:33:18.220
DIAMOND: Let me put
a different wrinkle

01:33:18.220 --> 01:33:20.560
on the interpretation
of the household sector

01:33:20.560 --> 01:33:22.480
and the word, business.

01:33:22.480 --> 01:33:24.783
From the employment
point of view,

01:33:24.783 --> 01:33:26.200
there's a great
deal of employment

01:33:26.200 --> 01:33:28.390
that goes on with
small business.

01:33:28.390 --> 01:33:33.400
And a lot of small business
is financed by banks,

01:33:33.400 --> 01:33:37.750
and is financed by drawing on--
there's the household link--

01:33:37.750 --> 01:33:41.200
their own wealth, the wealth
of families and friends.

01:33:41.200 --> 01:33:46.000
And what we have been seeing
in this great recession

01:33:46.000 --> 01:33:50.230
is that employment
growth by small business

01:33:50.230 --> 01:33:54.880
relative to employment growth
by large business is way down.

01:33:54.880 --> 01:33:59.200
And the cyclic behavior of the
two generally is different.

01:33:59.200 --> 01:34:02.810
And what we're seeing
now is an unusual amount,

01:34:02.810 --> 01:34:04.960
which I think is connected
to exactly what Bob

01:34:04.960 --> 01:34:09.550
was talking about, the financial
stretch-out of households

01:34:09.550 --> 01:34:10.900
and of banks.

01:34:10.900 --> 01:34:14.920
I think that affects the
financing not of big business,

01:34:14.920 --> 01:34:18.670
but of small business
and in terms of hiring.

01:34:18.670 --> 01:34:21.630
That's an important
part of the story.

01:34:21.630 --> 01:34:23.920
ACEMOGLU: Since we
are running late

01:34:23.920 --> 01:34:26.650
and we don't want to eat too
much more into the lunch time,

01:34:26.650 --> 01:34:30.250
I think we're going to have
only time for one more question.

01:34:30.250 --> 01:34:35.455
Sorry for the remaining people
who wanted to ask questions.

01:34:35.455 --> 01:34:37.580
AUDIENCE: Hopefully this
is worth your while, then.

01:34:37.580 --> 01:34:39.920
I'm Doug Carr, a Sloan grad.

01:34:39.920 --> 01:34:42.680
Obviously the issue of
the size of government

01:34:42.680 --> 01:34:45.340
is a huge controversy today.

01:34:45.340 --> 01:34:47.900
There are studies
and economics--

01:34:47.900 --> 01:34:52.220
Barrow, I believe, has found
that larger size of government

01:34:52.220 --> 01:34:56.540
relative to the economy has
associated with it a lower

01:34:56.540 --> 01:34:58.290
rate of long-term growth.

01:34:58.290 --> 01:35:02.240
And I think there may be other
studies that refute his study.

01:35:02.240 --> 01:35:04.460
Within the field
of growth theory,

01:35:04.460 --> 01:35:07.070
is there a framework for
analyzing this issue?

01:35:10.440 --> 01:35:14.040
SOLOW: Certainly a
framework for analyzing it.

01:35:14.040 --> 01:35:19.470
The difficulty in dealing
with associations like that

01:35:19.470 --> 01:35:24.090
is to translate it into a causal
mechanism one way or another.

01:35:24.090 --> 01:35:26.310
It is of course
perfectly possible

01:35:26.310 --> 01:35:32.460
that large government budgets
waste resources and affect

01:35:32.460 --> 01:35:34.030
the level of output that way.

01:35:34.030 --> 01:35:37.050
It's also possible that
slow-growing economies

01:35:37.050 --> 01:35:40.500
tend to depend more on
governmental intervention

01:35:40.500 --> 01:35:42.450
than fast-growing ones.

01:35:42.450 --> 01:35:45.450
My own feeling
about this is that

01:35:45.450 --> 01:35:49.200
what's more important
to begin with is

01:35:49.200 --> 01:35:54.510
to talk less about growth
and more about levels.

01:35:54.510 --> 01:35:58.080
One could easily make
a sensible argument

01:35:58.080 --> 01:36:02.310
one way or another about
the size of government

01:36:02.310 --> 01:36:07.080
relative to the economy and
the efficiency of the economy.

01:36:07.080 --> 01:36:11.100
But that's a matter of a
level change, not a change

01:36:11.100 --> 01:36:16.080
in something that's potentially
part of a steady-state rate

01:36:16.080 --> 01:36:17.260
of growth.

01:36:17.260 --> 01:36:23.040
So I tend to be suspicious of
all simple generalizations that

01:36:23.040 --> 01:36:30.150
go from some current
fraction to an implication

01:36:30.150 --> 01:36:33.480
about steady-state or
near-steady-state rates

01:36:33.480 --> 01:36:34.740
of growth.

01:36:34.740 --> 01:36:37.440
DIAMOND: Bob has--
if I can characterize

01:36:37.440 --> 01:36:42.240
that-- says he's
skeptical about an answer

01:36:42.240 --> 01:36:45.700
to that question,
Barrow's or the opposite.

01:36:45.700 --> 01:36:48.970
I'm skeptical about the
value of the question.

01:36:48.970 --> 01:36:53.290
And this also relates with
what Esther was saying.

01:36:53.290 --> 01:36:57.280
Esther was saying, more
aid, less aid, wrong debate.

01:36:57.280 --> 01:36:59.350
What we want is good aid.

01:36:59.350 --> 01:37:02.390
More government, less
government, wrong debate.

01:37:02.390 --> 01:37:04.240
There's good things
the government spends

01:37:04.240 --> 01:37:06.640
on that helps, if you'll
excuse the expression,

01:37:06.640 --> 01:37:11.530
economic growth, or
efficiency in the economy.

01:37:11.530 --> 01:37:14.170
And you do more of it,
then the level goes up,

01:37:14.170 --> 01:37:16.030
and that's growth.

01:37:16.030 --> 01:37:19.360
Regulation-- is it more
regulation or less regulation?

01:37:19.360 --> 01:37:24.370
Now, Bob was saying it's the
quality of the regulation.

01:37:24.370 --> 01:37:26.470
I was a little
surprised that dropping

01:37:26.470 --> 01:37:31.270
the regulation on leverage,
which you referred to

01:37:31.270 --> 01:37:37.990
as a policy lapse, isn't in the
category of a bad deregulation.

01:37:37.990 --> 01:37:40.780
Not that the whole
system was deregulated,

01:37:40.780 --> 01:37:42.880
if that's what the term
means, but I think,

01:37:42.880 --> 01:37:45.715
on regulations, there were
good and bad ones, on aid,

01:37:45.715 --> 01:37:47.590
there's good and bad,
on government spending,

01:37:47.590 --> 01:37:48.970
there's good and bad.

01:37:48.970 --> 01:37:51.310
And the idea that
you look at the whole

01:37:51.310 --> 01:37:53.710
and then move from
that, somehow,

01:37:53.710 --> 01:37:56.830
into policy and politics
seems, to me, misguided.

01:37:59.530 --> 01:38:02.710
SOLOW: Let me add
one more thing.

01:38:02.710 --> 01:38:05.630
This is what they call a
teachable moment, I think.

01:38:05.630 --> 01:38:07.430
[APPLAUSE]

01:38:08.780 --> 01:38:10.070
AUDIENCE: Teaching me?

01:38:10.070 --> 01:38:10.860
SOLOW: No, no, no.

01:38:10.860 --> 01:38:12.380
No, no, no.

01:38:12.380 --> 01:38:13.610
No, teaching the world.

01:38:13.610 --> 01:38:15.330
[LAUGHTER]

01:38:17.916 --> 01:38:22.190
A government that
spends money, which

01:38:22.190 --> 01:38:28.280
would appear as a larger
government, supporting higher

01:38:28.280 --> 01:38:32.870
education, supporting
technological innovation,

01:38:32.870 --> 01:38:36.830
supporting R&amp;D generally,
certainly has a prayer--

01:38:36.830 --> 01:38:38.960
more than a prayer--
a likelihood

01:38:38.960 --> 01:38:43.890
of affecting the steady
state rate of growth.

01:38:43.890 --> 01:38:49.670
A government that botches food
and drug regulation or that

01:38:49.670 --> 01:38:53.960
spends more or less will
affect the current efficiency

01:38:53.960 --> 01:38:59.190
of the economy, but won't affect
the rate-- to that extent,

01:38:59.190 --> 01:39:00.920
I think that's
what Peter had mind

01:39:00.920 --> 01:39:04.770
by saying he disagreed
with the question.

01:39:04.770 --> 01:39:05.840
ACEMOGLU: Bob Hall.

01:39:05.840 --> 01:39:08.110
HALL: Okay, great question.

01:39:08.110 --> 01:39:10.010
All right, so as
it happens, I've

01:39:10.010 --> 01:39:13.280
done research on this question,
published a paper on it,

01:39:13.280 --> 01:39:20.210
which agrees extremely strongly
with both Bob and Peter.

01:39:20.210 --> 01:39:22.610
In the first place, it
was in a levels framework,

01:39:22.610 --> 01:39:24.050
which I strongly agree with.

01:39:24.050 --> 01:39:25.560
What matters is the level.

01:39:25.560 --> 01:39:28.130
Growth is just a way
you get to the level.

01:39:28.130 --> 01:39:29.630
Second, it examines
this question

01:39:29.630 --> 01:39:35.810
of how government relates
to economic success.

01:39:35.810 --> 01:39:39.950
And the answer is, very
clearly, size is not important.

01:39:39.950 --> 01:39:41.450
And you can see
that in Scandinavia.

01:39:41.450 --> 01:39:43.605
Scandinavia has governments
that historically

01:39:43.605 --> 01:39:45.230
have been very involved
in the economy,

01:39:45.230 --> 01:39:48.170
but has achieved
excellent results.

01:39:48.170 --> 01:39:51.650
And then you see
some economies that

01:39:51.650 --> 01:39:55.250
have very small governments
that have terrible results.

01:39:55.250 --> 01:39:58.670
What shows through
unambiguously is competence.

01:39:58.670 --> 01:40:00.560
What we want is a
government that knows

01:40:00.560 --> 01:40:03.860
what it's doing, everywhere.

01:40:03.860 --> 01:40:08.330
Now, luckily, that's
pretty much what we have.

01:40:08.330 --> 01:40:13.160
The US does have-- it stands at
the very top in terms of output

01:40:13.160 --> 01:40:18.260
per worker, and generally,
with a government that

01:40:18.260 --> 01:40:22.580
does a good job of providing
an environment in which

01:40:22.580 --> 01:40:27.607
private activity can flourish
with the government being

01:40:27.607 --> 01:40:28.940
assigned the right things to do.

01:40:28.940 --> 01:40:31.940
There's a few exceptions,
like the post office.

01:40:31.940 --> 01:40:36.350
But generally, this
country gets things right.

01:40:36.350 --> 01:40:38.640
And if you go to another
country and see governments

01:40:38.640 --> 01:40:40.390
floundering, then you
can appreciate that.

01:40:43.580 --> 01:40:46.310
ACEMOGLU: Okay I think I
would like to thank the four

01:40:46.310 --> 01:40:47.660
panelists again for--

01:40:47.660 --> 01:40:50.110
[APPLAUSE]

